var e=JSON.parse('[[2001,"import type is used to import only types, not values. Unlike an ordinary import, it generates no code in JavaScript at compile time, which reduces bundle size and improves performance. This is especially useful together with build tools such as Webpack, for better tree shaking.","What is import type?\\nIn TypeScript `import type` is syntax that lets you import only types (interfaces, types, classes as types) without including the corresponding values in the resulting JavaScript code. An ordinary `import` brings in both types and values, which can lead to unnecessary code in the bundle.\\n\\nThe main differences\\n\\n- Code generation: `import type` is removed entirely at compile time, whereas an ordinary import leaves code behind for the values.\\n- Tree shaking: using `import type` helps build tools (Webpack, for example) optimize the bundle better by removing unused imports.\\n- Safety: `import type` guarantees that you do not use the imported module as a value, which prevents accidental errors.\\n\\nA code example\\n\\nIn this example `import type { User }` will add no code to the JavaScript, while `import { greet }` will. If we had used only `import type` for `User`, the `greet` function would not have been available.\\n\\nWhen to apply it\\nUse `import type` for every import that is needed only for types (interfaces, types, class types). This improves build performance and reduces bundle size, especially in large projects with many types.\\n\\nConclusion: `import type` is a tool for optimizing TypeScript projects that helps you avoid unnecessary code in JavaScript and improves tree shaking. Apply it everywhere you import only types, in order to make your code cleaner and more efficient.","// types.ts\\nexport interface User {\\n  name: string;\\n  age: number;\\n}\\n\\nexport function greet(user: User) {\\n  console.log(`Hello, ${user.name}`);\\n}\\n\\n// app.ts\\n// An ordinary import — brings in both the type and the function\\nimport { User, greet } from \'./types\';\\n\\n// import type — brings in only the type\\nimport type { User } from \'./types\';\\n\\n// Using the type\\nconst user: User = { name: \'Alice\', age: 30 };\\ngreet(user); // The greet function has to be imported separately"],[2002,"Record is a built-in utility type in TypeScript that creates an object type whose keys have the type K and whose values have the type V. It is convenient for creating dictionaries or mappings when you need to guarantee that every key of a particular set has a value of the same type. For example, `Record<string, number>` creates an object with string keys and numeric values.","What is Record in TypeScript?\\nRecord is one of TypeScript\'s built-in utility types, which lets you create typed objects with a fixed set of keys and a single value type. It is especially useful when you need to describe a data structure resembling a dictionary or associative array where all the values have the same type.\\n\\nThe syntax and its use\\nRecord takes two parameters: the type of the keys (usually string, number or a union of strings) and the type of the values. For example:\\n\\nIf the keys are known in advance, you can use a union of types for strict typing:\\n\\nPractical use\\nRecord is often applied for configurations, status mappings, form handling and any scenario requiring strict typing of objects with dynamic keys. It helps you avoid errors caused by incorrect value types or missing keys.\\n\\nConclusion\\nRecord is a powerful tool for creating typed dictionaries in TypeScript. It is worth using when you need to guarantee that all of an object\'s values have one type and that the keys correspond to a particular set, which increases the reliability and readability of your code.","type UserRoles = Record<string, string>;\\nconst roles: UserRoles = {\\n  admin: \'full_access\',\\n  user: \'limited_access\'\\n};\\n\\ntype Role = \'admin\' | \'user\' | \'guest\';\\ntype RolePermissions = Record<Role, string[]>;\\nconst permissions: RolePermissions = {\\n  admin: [\'read\', \'write\', \'delete\'],\\n  user: [\'read\'],\\n  guest: []\\n};"],[2003,"React.FC (or React.FunctionComponent) is a type for functional components in React with TypeScript. Historically it added children to the props automatically, which led to problems: components that took no children accepted them anyway, and it also did not support generics. Nowadays it is recommended to state children explicitly or to use types without React.FC.","What is React.FC?\\nReact.FC (or React.FunctionComponent) is a type built into React for typing functional components with TypeScript. It simplifies declaring a component by adding children to the props automatically and returning JSX.Element.\\n\\nThe historical problems with typing children\\nThe main problem with React.FC was that it added children to the props implicitly, even when the component was not supposed to accept them. This led to errors where a developer accidentally passed child elements to a component that did not handle them. For example:\\n\\nIn addition, React.FC did not support generics, which complicated creating reusable components with different prop types. It also did not allow defaultProps to be used with correct typing.\\n\\nThe modern approach\\nFrom React 18 onwards it is recommended to avoid React.FC and state children explicitly in the props if they are needed. This makes code more predictable and flexible. An example:\\n\\nThis approach eliminates the implicit addition of children and lets you control the types better.\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact.FC was useful for quick typing, but because of the problems with children and its limitations it is better to declare props explicitly. This matters especially on large projects, where precise types are critical for preventing errors.","// A component that expects no children\\nconst Header: React.FC = () => <h1>Title</h1>;\\n\\n// No error arises, even though children are not wanted\\n<Header>Some text</Header>;\\n\\n// Stating children explicitly\\ninterface HeaderProps {\\n  title: string;\\n  children?: React.ReactNode;\\n}\\n\\nconst Header = ({ title, children }: HeaderProps) => {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>{title}</h1>\\n      {children}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n};"],[2004,"The public modifier before a constructor parameter in TypeScript creates and initializes a public class property with the same name automatically. This shortens the code by sparing you the manual declaration of the field and the assignment of the value. This syntax is called a parameter property and is available only in TypeScript, not in JavaScript.","What a parameter property is in TypeScript\\nWhen you write `constructor(public name: string)`, TypeScript creates a public `name` property on the class automatically and assigns the value it was given. This is equivalent to declaring the field by hand and assigning it in the constructor\'s body.\\n\\nA comparison\\nWithout the syntactic sugar:\\n\\nWith a parameter property:\\n\\nBoth variants are identical after compilation. The `private`, `protected` and `readonly` modifiers work the same way.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- In classes with simple constructors, where fields need to be declared and initialized quickly.\\n- In DI containers (Angular, NestJS, for example) for injecting dependencies through the constructor.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse parameter properties to reduce boilerplate when a class field corresponds directly to a constructor parameter. This improves readability and reduces the chance of errors in manual assignment.","class Person {\\n  public name: string;\\n  constructor(name: string) {\\n    this.name = name;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass Person {\\n  constructor(public name: string) {}\\n}"],[2005,"Record is a built-in utility type in TypeScript that creates an object type whose keys have the type K and whose values have the type V. For example, `Record<string, number>` means an object with any string keys and numeric values. It is a convenient alternative to index signatures when you need to describe a dictionary or a map.","What is Record in TypeScript?\\nRecord is a built-in utility type that lets you describe an object with arbitrary keys of type K and values of type V. It is often used to create types representing dictionaries, maps or configurations where the keys are not known in advance but the values are all of the same type.\\n\\nThe syntax and examples\\nThe basic syntax is `Record<KeyType, ValueType>`. KeyType is usually string, number or symbol, and ValueType is any type.\\n\\nA comparison with index signatures\\nRecord is syntactic sugar over index signatures. Instead of `{ [key: string]: number }` you can write `Record<string, number>`. Record is more concise and readable, especially when working with union types of keys.\\n\\nPractical use\\nRecord is often used to:\\n- Describe configurations with dynamic keys\\n- Create types for API responses where the keys are identifiers\\n- Implement the \\"registry\\" or \\"factory\\" pattern\\n\\nConclusion\\nRecord is a powerful, convenient tool for typing objects with arbitrary keys. It is worth applying when you need to describe a dictionary, a map or any structure where the keys are dynamic and the values are all of the same type. It makes code more readable and safer than hand-written index signatures.","// A simple example: a dictionary of strings\\nconst dict: Record<string, string> = {\\n  name: \\"Alice\\",\\n  role: \\"developer\\"\\n};\\n\\n// With numeric keys\\nconst scores: Record<number, number> = {\\n  1: 95,\\n  2: 87\\n};\\n\\n// With a union of keys\\ntype Status = \\"active\\" | \\"inactive\\";\\nconst users: Record<Status, number> = {\\n  active: 10,\\n  inactive: 3\\n};\\n\\n// An index signature\\ninterface StringNumberMap {\\n  [key: string]: number;\\n}\\n\\n// The equivalent through Record\\ntype StringNumberMap = Record<string, number>;\\n\\n// An example: an application\'s configuration\\ninterface AppConfig {\\n  port: number;\\n  debug: boolean;\\n}\\n\\nconst config: Record<string, AppConfig> = {\\n  production: { port: 8080, debug: false },\\n  development: { port: 3000, debug: true }\\n};"],[2006,"With strictNullChecks enabled, TypeScript prevents null or undefined from being assigned to variables unless that is allowed explicitly. For working with values that may be null, union types (`string | null`, for example) and type guards are used. Control over reassigning types is achieved with readonly, const and strict typing, which prevents a variable\'s type from being changed by accident.","How TypeScript helps you control null and the reassignment of types\\nTypeScript provides powerful tools for working safely with null and undefined, and for controlling changes to variables\' types. This matters especially for preventing common runtime errors caused by unexpected null values or by the unintended change of a data type.\\n\\nControlling null and undefined\\nThe main mechanism is the strictNullChecks flag in tsconfig.json. When it is enabled, TypeScript does not allow null or undefined to be assigned to a variable unless its type explicitly permits those values. For example:\\n\\nTo allow null you have to use a union type:\\n\\nFor working safely with such variables, type guards are applied — a check for null, for example:\\n\\nYou can also use the optional chaining (?.) and nullish coalescing (??) operators:\\n\\nControlling the reassignment of types\\nTypeScript prevents a variable\'s type from being changed by accident through strict typing. If a variable is declared with a particular type, a value of a different type cannot be assigned to it without an explicit cast:\\n\\nFor immutable variables the const keyword is used, which forbids reassignment:\\n\\nYou can also use the readonly modifier for the properties of objects or for arrays:\\n\\nConclusion\\nWith strictNullChecks, union types, type guards and the readonly modifier, TypeScript provides strict control over null values and the reassignment of types. This makes code more predictable and safer, especially on large projects where such errors are hard to track down by hand.","let name: string = \'Alice\';\\nname = null; // Error: Type \'null\' is not assignable to type \'string\'\\n\\nlet name: string | null = \'Alice\';\\nname = null; // OK\\n\\nfunction greet(name: string | null) {\\n  if (name !== null) {\\n    console.log(name.toUpperCase()); // TypeScript knows that name is a string\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst city = user?.address?.city ?? \'Unknown\';\\n\\nlet count: number = 5;\\ncount = \'hello\'; // Error: Type \'string\' is not assignable to type \'number\'\\n\\nconst pi: number = 3.14;\\npi = 3.14159; // Error: Cannot assign to \'pi\' because it is a constant\\n\\ninterface Config {\\n  readonly apiUrl: string;\\n}\\nconst config: Config = { apiUrl: \'https://api.example.com\' };\\nconfig.apiUrl = \'new-url\'; // Error: Cannot assign to \'apiUrl\' because it is a read-only property"],[2007,"An intersection type (&) creates a type that has to satisfy all the combined types at once. A union type (|) creates a type that may be one of the given types. Intersection is used to combine several types into one, union to state alternatives.","What are intersection and union types?\\nIn TypeScript intersection types and union types are powerful tools for working with types. An intersection, denoted by the `&` symbol, lets you create a new type that includes all the properties and methods of all the given types. A union, denoted by the `|` symbol, creates a type that may be one of the listed types.\\n\\nIntersection types (&)\\nAn intersection type is used when an object has to conform to several types at once. For example, if you have a `User` type and an `Admin` type, the intersection `User & Admin` will contain all the properties of both.\\n\\nIn this example `SuperUser` must have every field from `User` and from `Admin`. If some field is missing, TypeScript reports an error.\\n\\nUnion types (|)\\nA union type is used when a value may be one of several types. For example, a function may accept a string or a number.\\n\\nWhen working with a union type, TypeScript lets you access only the properties common to all the types. To reach the unique properties you have to use type narrowing.\\n\\nPractical use\\nIntersection types are often used to combine interfaces — for example, when creating extended configurations or mixing functionality. Union types are useful for handling different data formats, such as API responses that may be successful or erroneous.\\n\\nConclusion\\nIntersection and union types are fundamental TypeScript concepts that help you create flexible, safe types. Intersection suits combining several types into one; union suits working with alternative types. Use them to improve the readability and reliability of your code.","interface User {\\n  name: string;\\n  age: number;\\n}\\n\\ninterface Admin {\\n  role: string;\\n  permissions: string[];\\n}\\n\\ntype SuperUser = User & Admin;\\n\\nconst user: SuperUser = {\\n  name: \'Alice\',\\n  age: 30,\\n  role: \'admin\',\\n  permissions: [\'read\', \'write\']\\n};\\n\\nfunction printId(id: string | number) {\\n  console.log(\'ID:\', id);\\n}\\n\\nprintId(\'abc\'); // OK\\nprintId(123);   // OK\\n// printId(true); // Error: boolean is not part of the union"],[2008,"A service in Angular is a class with the @Injectable decorator, used to move shared logic (working with an API, holding state, calculations) out of components. It is registered in the providers of a module or component and injected through the constructor. Services make code reusable and testable and follow the single-responsibility principle.","A service in Angular is an ordinary TypeScript class marked with the `@Injectable` decorator. A service\'s main purpose is to move business logic, data handling and shared functions outside components, so that the components stay \\"thin\\" and deal only with presentation.\\n\\nCreating a service\\nA service is created with the Angular CLI using the command `ng generate service my-service`. By hand you need to define a class with the `@Injectable` decorator and state at which level it will be available (`providedIn: \'root\'` for a global singleton, for example).\\n\\nUse through Dependency Injection\\nAngular uses a dependency injection mechanism. The service is registered with the providers (the root injector, a module or a component), after which it can be requested in the constructor of any component or other service.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- Working with HTTP (HttpClient) — API calls.\\n- Shared state (a shopping cart, for example).\\n- Logging, error handling, caching.\\n- Exchanging data between unrelated components.\\n\\nConclusion: services in Angular are the main way of organizing logic that is not tied to a template. Using them makes code cleaner and simplifies testing and reuse. It is recommended to move any logic into services except direct work with the DOM or the handling of view events.","import { Injectable } from \'@angular/core\';\\n\\n@Injectable({\\n  providedIn: \'root\' // the service will be a singleton for the whole application\\n})\\nexport class DataService {\\n  private data: string[] = [];\\n\\n  getData(): string[] {\\n    return this.data;\\n  }\\n\\n  addData(item: string): void {\\n    this.data.push(item);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nimport { Component } from \'@angular/core\';\\nimport { DataService } from \'./data.service\';\\n\\n@Component({\\n  selector: \'app-example\',\\n  template: `<ul><li *ngFor=\\"let item of items\\">{{item}}</li></ul>`\\n})\\nexport class ExampleComponent {\\n  items: string[];\\n\\n  constructor(private dataService: DataService) {\\n    this.items = this.dataService.getData();\\n  }\\n}"],[2009,"A class field is made immutable with the readonly modifier. Such a field can be initialized either at its declaration or in the class\'s constructor. After that its value cannot be changed. This helps protect data from accidental modification and makes code more predictable.","What are readonly fields in TypeScript?\\nThe `readonly` modifier in TypeScript lets you make a class property immutable once it has been initialized. This means that after a value has been assigned to the field, it cannot be changed. The approach is useful for creating configurations, constants or any data that must not change during an object\'s life.\\n\\nWhere can a readonly field be initialized?\\nA readonly field can be initialized in only two places:\\n- Directly at the field\'s declaration\\n- In the class\'s constructor\\n\\nAn attempt to change a readonly field in any other method of the class will cause a compile error.\\n\\nA code example\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse readonly for fields that have to stay unchanged after the object is created. This increases the reliability of your code and prevents accidental changes to important data, especially on large projects with many developers.","class Config {\\n  readonly apiUrl: string = \'https://api.example.com\'; // initialization at the declaration\\n  readonly timeout: number;\\n\\n  constructor(timeout: number) {\\n    this.timeout = timeout; // initialization in the constructor\\n  }\\n\\n  updateTimeout(newTimeout: number): void {\\n    // this.timeout = newTimeout; // Error! A readonly field cannot be changed\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst config = new Config(5000);\\nconsole.log(config.apiUrl); // \'https://api.example.com\'\\nconsole.log(config.timeout); // 5000"],[2010,"Factory Method is a pattern that defines an interface for creating an object but lets subclasses change the type of object that gets created. Factory (or Abstract Factory) is a pattern that provides an interface for creating families of related objects without specifying their concrete classes. The main difference: Factory Method uses inheritance and delegates the creation of the object to subclasses, whereas Factory uses composition and delegates creation to a whole factory object.","An introduction to the patterns\\nThe Factory Method and Factory patterns are often confused, although they solve different problems. Both are creational patterns, but they differ in their level of abstraction and in how they delegate the creation of objects.\\n\\nFactory Method\\nThis pattern defines an interface for creating a single object but lets subclasses decide which concrete class to instantiate. It uses inheritance: the base class declares a factory method and the subclasses override it.\\n\\nFactory (Abstract Factory)\\nThe Factory pattern provides an interface for creating families of related objects. It uses composition: the client receives a factory object and calls its methods to create products.\\n\\nThe key differences\\n\\n- The goal: Factory Method creates one object, Factory a family of objects.\\n- The implementation: Factory Method uses inheritance and overriding a method; Factory uses composition and a separate factory class.\\n- Flexibility: Factory is more flexible, since it lets you swap out whole families of products, but it is harder to implement.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse Factory Method when you need to delegate the creation of a single object to subclasses, and Factory when groups of related objects have to be created with a guarantee of their compatibility.","abstract class Document {\\n    abstract createPage(): Page;\\n    open() {\\n        const page = this.createPage();\\n        page.render();\\n    }\\n}\\nclass Resume extends Document {\\n    createPage(): Page {\\n        return new SkillPage();\\n    }\\n}\\n\\ninterface GUIFactory {\\n    createButton(): Button;\\n    createCheckbox(): Checkbox;\\n}\\nclass WinFactory implements GUIFactory {\\n    createButton(): Button { return new WinButton(); }\\n    createCheckbox(): Checkbox { return new WinCheckbox(); }\\n}"],[2011,"Excessive responsibility is the situation where a method or class performs several unrelated tasks. It violates the single-responsibility principle (SRP), reduces readability and complicates testing and maintenance. Such code is hard to change without the risk of breaking other parts of the system.","What is excessive responsibility?\\nExcessive responsibility (a violation of the Single Responsibility Principle) arises when one class or method takes on the performance of several logically unrelated tasks. For example, a class may process data, save it to a database and send email notifications all at once. This makes code rigid and hard to understand and change.\\n\\nAn example of the problem\\nConsider a class responsible both for calculating salaries and for sending a report:\\n\\nHere three different responsibilities are mixed into one class. If the report\'s format changes, you will have to change a class that is also responsible for calculating salaries — which increases the risk of errors.\\n\\nHow do you fix it?\\nSplit the class into several, each with its own clear task:\\n\\nConclusion\\nObserving the single-responsibility principle makes code more modular, testable and adaptable to change. Apply this approach whenever you notice that a class or method is starting to perform more than one logical task.","class EmployeeService {\\n  calculateSalary(employee) {\\n    // complex calculations\\n  }\\n  sendReport() {\\n    // sending an email\\n  }\\n  saveToDatabase() {\\n    // SQL queries\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nclass SalaryCalculator {\\n  calculate(employee) { /* ... */ }\\n}\\nclass ReportSender {\\n  send(report) { /* ... */ }\\n}\\nclass EmployeeRepository {\\n  save(employee) { /* ... */ }\\n}"],[2012,"A namespace in TypeScript is a way of grouping code logically that helps avoid name conflicts in the global scope. Namespaces let you gather related functions, classes and interfaces under one name. They are used to organize code in large projects, especially when ES6 modules are not in use.","What is a namespace in TypeScript?\\nA namespace is a TypeScript construct that lets you group related entities (classes, interfaces, functions) under one name. This helps avoid polluting the global scope and name conflicts, especially on large projects.\\n\\nHow do namespaces work?\\nNamespaces are created with the `namespace` keyword. Everything declared inside is private by default unless it is exported with `export`. To reach a namespace\'s members the syntax `NamespaceName.Member` is used.\\n\\nWhen should you use a namespace?\\nNamespaces are useful in the following cases:\\n- When you are working with the global context (in the browser without modules, for example).\\n- To organize code in large projects where ES6 modules are not used.\\n- To create libraries that will be included through `<script>` tags.\\n\\nThe difference from modules\\nIn modern TypeScript it is recommended to use ES6 modules (import/export) rather than namespaces. Modules provide better isolation, support from build tools and more predictable behaviour. Namespaces remain relevant for backward compatibility and for specific scenarios.\\n\\nConclusion: a namespace is a tool for organizing code in TypeScript that helps avoid name conflicts. They are worth applying in projects where ES6 modules are unavailable, or for creating global libraries, but modern development prefers modules.","namespace Geometry {\\n  export interface Point {\\n    x: number;\\n    y: number;\\n  }\\n\\n  export function distance(p1: Point, p2: Point): number {\\n    return Math.sqrt((p2.x - p1.x) ** 2 + (p2.y - p1.y) ** 2);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst p1: Geometry.Point = { x: 0, y: 0 };\\nconst p2: Geometry.Point = { x: 3, y: 4 };\\nconsole.log(Geometry.distance(p1, p2)); // 5"],[2013,"The infer keyword is used in TypeScript\'s conditional types to declare a type variable that is inferred from the type being checked. It lets you extract and reuse parts of types — a function\'s return type or an array\'s element type, for example. Infer works only inside an extends with the ternary operator and cannot be used outside conditional types.","What is infer in TypeScript?\\nThe `infer` keyword is a powerful TypeScript tool that lets you declare type variables inside conditional types. It is used to capture and then use a part of the type being checked in the condition. Without `infer` you would not be able to extract inner types from complex structures dynamically.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nInfer is applied only in the construct `T extends U ? X : Y`, where `U` contains `infer`. When TypeScript checks whether `T` matches the pattern `U`, it infers a type for the variable declared through `infer` and substitutes it into the `X` branch.\\n\\nExamples of use\\nConsider extracting an array\'s element type:\\n\\nAnother example is obtaining a function\'s return type:\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\nInfer is widely used in TypeScript\'s built-in utility types such as `ReturnType`, `Parameters`, `ConstructorParameters` and `InstanceType`. Developers also apply it to create their own advanced types — for extracting types from a Promise or from React components, for example.\\n\\nConclusion\\nInfer is a key mechanism for working with conditional types in TypeScript, letting you extract and reuse parts of types dynamically. It is indispensable when creating flexible, type-safe abstractions, especially in libraries and frameworks where types have to be manipulated at a high level.","type ArrayElement<T> = T extends (infer U)[] ? U : never;\\ntype Num = ArrayElement<number[]>; // number\\ntype Str = ArrayElement<string[]>; // string\\n\\ntype ReturnType<T> = T extends (...args: any[]) => infer R ? R : never;\\ntype Fn = (x: number) => string;\\ntype Result = ReturnType<Fn>; // string"],[2014,"JSX (JavaScript XML) is an extension to the JavaScript syntax that lets you write HTML-like code right inside JavaScript. It is used in React to describe what the interface should look like. JSX makes creating components easier by bringing the markup and the logic together in one place.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nUnder the hood JSX is turned into function calls, for example:","JSX is a syntax extension to JavaScript, developed specifically for React. It lets you write code resembling HTML that is embedded in JavaScript. Although it looks like HTML, it is not HTML: JSX has rules and limitations of its own.\\n\\nWhy is JSX used?\\n\\n- Readability: the code becomes clearer, since the markup and the logic sit side by side.\\n- Flexibility: JSX lets you use JavaScript inside the markup by means of `{}`.\\n- Efficiency: React uses JSX to create the virtual DOM, optimizing the process of updating the interface.\\n\\nHow does JSX work?\\nJSX is nothing more than syntactic sugar. Before the browser executes it, it is compiled into `React.createElement()` calls. For example:\\n\\nCompiles into:\\n\\nJSX\'s main characteristics\\n\\n- Embedding JavaScript\\nInside JSX you can use JavaScript expressions enclosed in `{}`:\\n\\n- Attributes\\nJSX uses attributes that resemble HTML\'s but with JavaScript\'s peculiarities. Instead of `class`, for example, `className` is used, and for events — `onClick`.\\n\\n- Child elements\\nYou can pass elements and components as children:\\n\\n- Fragments\\nTo group elements without an unnecessary container, `React.Fragment` or `<>` is used.\\n\\nAn example of using JSX in a component\\n\\nThe rules of JSX\\n\\n- A single parent element: every JSX block must be wrapped in one root element.\\n- camelCase for attributes: `onClick`, `className`, for example.\\n- Closing tags: every element must be closed, even the self-contained ones:\\n\\nJSX and security\\nBy default JSX prevents malicious code from being executed, by escaping every JavaScript insertion. For example:\\n\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- A convenient syntax for working with interfaces.\\n- Ease of integrating JavaScript.\\n- Clarity of code.\\n\\nThe disadvantages:\\n\\n- A compilation step is required (Babel).\\n- Beginners may find it harder to master than plain HTML.\\n\\nJSX is a tool for development in React that combines the convenience of writing markup with the capabilities of JavaScript. It makes code more expressive and more flexible, simplifying the process of building interfaces.","const element = <h1>Hello!</h1>;\\n\\nconst element = React.createElement(\'h1\', null, \'Hello!\');\\n\\nconst name = \\"World\\";\\nconst element = <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>;\\n\\nconst button = <button className=\\"primary\\" onClick={() => alert(\\"Click!\\")}>Click</button>;\\n\\nconst element = (\\n  <div>\\n    <h1>A heading</h1>\\n    <p>Some paragraph text</p>\\n  </div>\\n);\\n\\nconst element = (\\n  <>\\n    <h1>A heading</h1>\\n    <p>Some paragraph text</p>\\n  </>\\n);\\n\\nfunction Greeting(props) {\\n  return <h1>Hello, {props.name}!</h1>;\\n}\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <Greeting name=\\"World\\" />\\n      <Greeting name=\\"React\\" />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n<img src=\\"image.jpg\\" alt=\\"a picture\\" />\\n\\nconst userInput = \\"<script>alert(\'malicious code\')<\/script>\\";\\nconst element = <div>{userInput}</div>; // The insertion will be safe"],[2015,"Prop drilling is the process of passing data from a parent component down to its children through \\"props\\", even when the intermediate components do not use that data. It can complicate the code, especially when the components are deeply nested. The problem of prop drilling can be solved by using approaches such as the `Context API` or state-management libraries (Redux, for example).\\n\\nAn example:","In React data is passed from a parent down to its child components through props. Sometimes a situation arises where the data is needed by a component that sits deep in the hierarchy, but on the way to that component it has to be passed through several intermediate components that do not use the data themselves. That is what is called prop drilling.\\n\\nAn example of the prop-drilling problem\\n\\nIn this example the `Parent` and `Child` components merely \\"pass through\\" the `user` data without using it themselves. That makes the code less readable and increases the difficulty of refactoring.\\n\\nWhy is this a problem?\\n\\n- Redundant code: the intermediate components receive props they do not need.\\n- Difficulty of maintenance: when the structure of the components changes, every level has to be updated.\\n- Difficulty of scaling: with deep nesting or a great deal of data, managing the props becomes awkward.\\n\\nHow do you avoid prop drilling?\\n\\n- Context API\\nReact provides the `Context API` for passing data through the component tree, bypassing the intermediate levels. An example:\\n\\n- State management\\nUsing libraries such as Redux or Zustand helps you centralize the application\'s state and avoid passing data directly through props.\\n- Higher-order components (HOCs) and render props\\nThese approaches let you pass data only to where it is needed, without involving the intermediate components.\\n\\nWhen is prop drilling acceptable?\\nProp drilling can be a perfectly normal solution if the data is passed through 1–2 levels and the application\'s architecture stays simple. In large, complex applications, however, this approach becomes inefficient.\\n\\nProp drilling is a natural part of React, but as an application\'s complexity grows it is important to avoid excessive nesting of props. To solve the problem you can use the `Context API`, centralized state management or other architectural approaches that make passing data easier.","function App() {\\n  const user = { name: \\"Ivan\\" };\\n  return <Parent user={user} />;\\n}\\nfunction Parent({ user }) {\\n  return <Child user={user} />;\\n}\\nfunction Child({ user }) {\\n  return <Grandchild user={user} />;\\n}\\nfunction Grandchild({ user }) {\\n  return <h1>Hello, {user.name}!</h1>;\\n}\\n\\nimport React, { createContext, useContext } from \\"react\\";\\n\\nconst UserContext = createContext();\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  const user = { name: \\"Ivan\\" };\\n  return (\\n    <UserContext.Provider value={user}>\\n      <Parent />\\n    </UserContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  return <Child />;\\n}\\nfunction Child() {\\n  return <Grandchild />;\\n}\\nfunction Grandchild() {\\n  const user = useContext(UserContext);\\n  return <h1>Hello, {user.name}!</h1>;\\n}"],[2016,"In React `ref` is used to get direct access to DOM elements or to control them, and also to hold data that does not affect the component\'s re-rendering. With `ref`, for example, you can focus input elements, track the state of an animation or keep values between renders without updating them.","`ref` (short for reference) is a way of working with DOM elements or of keeping values between renders without their taking part in the process of updating the interface. In React a `ref` is created by means of the `useRef` function or the `React.createRef` method.\\n\\nWhen should you use a `ref`?\\n\\n- Access to DOM elements\\nWhen you need to control DOM elements directly — to set focus, to scroll elements or to change their dimensions, for example.\\n- Holding mutable data\\nWhen you need to keep a value that must not trigger a re-render (the ids of timers, for example).\\n- Integration with libraries\\nWhen a React component interacts with libraries that require direct access to the DOM (animation with third-party libraries, for example).\\n\\nAn example of using it to control the DOM\\n\\nAn example of holding a mutable value\\n\\nAn example of integrating with a library\\n\\nThe characteristics of using `ref`\\n\\n- It does not change React\'s data flow\\nUsing a `ref` bypasses React\'s mechanism, since it works with the DOM directly.\\n- Changes through `current`\\nA `ref`\'s value is held in the `ref.current` object. That value can be changed without triggering a re-render.\\n- Support for class components\\nFor class components `React.createRef` is used to create a ref:\\n\\nWhen should you not use a `ref`?\\n\\n- If the task can be solved through state or props.\\n- For managing data that ought to trigger a render.\\n- If direct interaction with the DOM is not required.\\n\\n`ref` is a tool for solving problems related to the DOM or to keeping values between renders. Its use should be justified, however, so as not to complicate the application\'s architecture.","import React, { useRef } from \\"react\\";\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  const divRef = useRef(null);\\n  const highlight = () => {\\n    divRef.current.style.backgroundColor = \\"yellow\\";\\n  };\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <div ref={divRef}>This is the element</div>\\n      <button onClick={highlight}>Highlight</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nimport React, { useRef, useState } from \\"react\\";\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const countRef = useRef(0);\\n  const [renderCount, setRenderCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  const increment = () => {\\n    countRef.current += 1; // Changing the value without a render\\n    console.log(\\"The current value:\\", countRef.current);\\n  };\\n\\n  const reRender = () => setRenderCount((prev) => prev + 1);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button onClick={increment}>Increment the counter</button>\\n      <button onClick={reRender}>Re-render</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nimport React, { useRef, useEffect } from \\"react\\";\\nimport Chart from \\"chart.js/auto\\";\\n\\nfunction ChartComponent() {\\n  const canvasRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    new Chart(canvasRef.current, {\\n      type: \\"bar\\",\\n      data: {\\n        labels: [\\"A\\", \\"B\\", \\"C\\"],\\n        datasets: [{ label: \\"Data\\", data: [10, 20, 30] }],\\n      },\\n    });\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return <canvas ref={canvasRef}></canvas>;\\n}\\n\\nclass MyComponent extends React.Component {\\n  constructor(props) {\\n    super(props);\\n    this.myRef = React.createRef();\\n  }\\n  componentDidMount() {\\n    console.log(this.myRef.current);\\n  }\\n  render() {\\n    return <div ref={this.myRef}>Hello!</div>;\\n  }\\n}"],[2017,"React is a JavaScript library for building interfaces. It lets developers build applications out of components that can be reused. Its key characteristics: the use of a virtual DOM for high performance, a unidirectional data flow, a component-based approach, and support for hooks for working with state and effects.\\n\\nAn example of a simple component:","React is a JavaScript library developed by Facebook in 2013, which is used to build user interfaces (UI). Unlike frameworks (Angular, for example), React is responsible only for the View in the MVC architecture.\\n\\nReact\'s key characteristics:\\n\\n- A component-based approach\\nReact lets you break an interface down into small independent parts (components) that can be reused. Components make code easier to develop and to maintain.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\n- The virtual DOM\\nReact uses a virtual DOM (a virtual representation of the DOM tree) in order to minimize operations on the real DOM, which can be expensive in performance terms. When the state changes, React updates only the parts of the page that have changed rather than the whole tree.\\n- A unidirectional data flow\\nData is passed from the top down (from parent components to their children) through properties (props). This makes the code predictable and easier to test.\\n\\nJSX\\nReact uses the JSX syntax, which lets you write HTML inside JavaScript. That makes building interfaces easier by bringing the markup and the logic together.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nState and hooks\\nReact offers flexible mechanisms for working with state. Hooks such as `useState` and `useEffect` let you add state and manage a component\'s lifecycle without using classes.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nReact Developer Tools\\nReact has powerful debugging tools that help you analyse components and their state.\\n\\nWhy is React popular?\\n\\n- Speed: thanks to the virtual DOM.\\n- Scalability: it is easy to build complex applications out of components.\\n- The community: a great many ready-made libraries and solutions.\\n- The tooling: a broad ecosystem, including Next.js for SSR and React Native for mobile applications.\\n\\nWhere is React used?\\nReact is used for:\\n- Single-page applications (SPAs).\\n- Complex interfaces (admin panels, for example).\\n- Mobile applications through React Native.\\n\\nAn example of a React application (a shopping cart):\\n\\nReact makes the development of dynamic, performant interfaces easier, which makes it the number-one choice for modern web applications.","function Button({ label }) {\\n  return <button>{label}</button>;\\n}\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <Button label=\\"Click me\\" />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Greeting() {\\n  const name = \\"World\\";\\n  return <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>;\\n}\\n\\nimport React, { useState } from \\"react\\";\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Counter: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction CartItem({ item, onRemove }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <span>{item.name}</span>\\n      <button onClick={() => onRemove(item.id)}>Remove</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction ShoppingCart() {\\n  const [items, setItems] = useState([\\n    { id: 1, name: \\"Apple\\" },\\n    { id: 2, name: \\"Banana\\" },\\n  ]);\\n\\n  const removeItem = (id) => setItems(items.filter((item) => item.id !== id));\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      {items.map((item) => (\\n        <CartItem key={item.id} item={item} onRemove={removeItem} />\\n      ))}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2018,"Components in React are an application\'s building blocks. Each component is an independent part of the user interface that can be reused and combined with other components. Components can be either functions or classes, and they return JSX to be displayed.","Components in React are separate parts of an interface that can be reused and combined in order to build complex applications. Each component can be represented either by a function or by a class (although functional components with hooks are the modern standard). A component\'s main job is to return JSX (JavaScript XML), which determines what the interface will look like.","function Welcome(props) {\\n   return <h1>Hello, {props.name}!</h1>;\\n}"],[2019,"\\"Props\\" (short for \\"properties\\") are a way of passing data from one component to another. They are immutable and let parent components pass information down to their children in order to configure their behaviour or their appearance.","\\"Props\\" are an object containing the data that is passed into a component when it is called. They are used to configure components and let parent components interact with their children. If you need to pass a user\'s name into a greeting component, for example, you can use `props` for that.","function Welcome(props) {\\n   return <h1>Hello, {props.name}!</h1>;\\n}\\n\\n// Using the component\\n<Welcome name=\\"Yulia\\" />"],[2020,"\\"Props\\" are data passed into a component from outside, whereas \\"state\\" is a component\'s internal data, which can change. Props are passed down by the parent component and are immutable, while state is managed inside the component itself and can change over time.","\\"Props\\" and \\"state\\" are two different ways of managing data in React. Props are passed into a component by its parent and cannot be changed inside the component; they are static. State, by contrast, is a component\'s internal data, which can change and which determines its current condition.","function Counter(props) {\\n   const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n   return (\\n       <div>\\n      <h2>{props.title}</h2>\\n           <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n           <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>\\n       </div>\\n   );\\n}"],[2021,"The Virtual DOM is an abstraction of the real DOM, which React uses in order to improve performance. Instead of updating the real DOM directly on every change of state, React first updates the virtual DOM and then compares it against the previous version, so as to minimize the number of operations on the real DOM. This process is called reconciliation.\\n\\nAn example:\\n- React updates the virtual DOM.\\n- It compares it against the previous state.\\n- It changes only the parts of the real DOM that have changed.","The Virtual DOM is a concept implemented in React, which is a lightweight copy of the real DOM. Instead of manipulating the real DOM directly on every change of state, React first updates the virtual DOM. After that React compares the virtual DOM against the previous state (this is called reconciliation) and computes the minimal changes that need to be made to the real DOM.\\n\\nWhy does the Virtual DOM matter?\\n\\n- Performance: the real DOM is a fairly expensive operation in performance terms, since any change requires the whole page to be recomputed and repainted. The virtual DOM helps minimize those operations by updating only the elements that really have changed.\\n- Optimizing updates: instead of repainting the whole interface, React updates only the elements that have been changed. That makes the interface run more smoothly and more quickly.\\n\\nHow does the Virtual DOM work in React?\\n\\n- Updating the state: when the user interacts with the application and the state changes, React first updates the virtual DOM, which is a copy of the real DOM\'s current state.\\n- Comparison (reconciliation): React then compares the virtual DOM\'s new state against the previous one. That comparison lets React work out exactly which elements on the page have changed.\\n- Updating the real DOM: once it has computed the differences, React minimizes the changes to the real DOM, applying only the updates that really are necessary, which improves performance considerably.\\n\\nAn example of the Virtual DOM at work\\nSuppose we have a component with state that changes when a button is clicked:\\n\\n- When the user clicks the button, the `count` state is updated.\\n- React updates the virtual DOM, where only the text in the `<p>` element changes.\\n- React then compares the new virtual DOM against the previous one and sees that only the text inside the `<p>` has changed.\\n- React updates the real DOM, changing only the text inside that element rather than repainting the whole page.\\n\\nThe advantages of the Virtual DOM:\\n\\n- Speed: updates to the real DOM require costly operations such as repainting elements, changing their state and changing the structure. The virtual DOM cuts the number of those operations down, making the application faster.\\n- Fewer bugs: the reconciliation between the virtual and the real DOM helps you avoid bugs related to a mismatch in the interface\'s state.\\n- Easier work with components: React keeps track of the components\' state automatically and updates only the parts of the UI that have been changed. That lets developers concentrate on a component\'s logic without worrying about complicated update optimization.\\n\\nAn explanation through an example:\\nSuppose we change a list:\\n\\n- When the user clicks the button, a new item is added to the list.\\n- React updates the virtual DOM, where a new `<li>` is added.\\n- Having compared the new virtual DOM against the previous one, React finds only one change — the addition of a new item to the list.\\n- The real DOM is updated with the addition of the new item alone.\\n\\nThe Virtual DOM is a key concept in React, which helps improve performance by minimizing operations on the real DOM. By means of the virtual DOM React updates only the parts of the interface that have changed, which makes applications faster and more responsive.","import React, { useState } from \\"react\\";\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n    const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n    return (\\n        <div>\\n            <p>{count}</p>\\n            <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>\\n        </div>\\n    );\\n}\\n\\nimport React, { useState } from \\"react\\";\\n\\nfunction ItemList() {\\n    const [items, setItems] = useState([\\"apple\\", \\"banana\\", \\"cherry\\"]);\\n\\n    const addItem = () => {\\n        setItems([...items, \\"orange\\"]);\\n    };\\n\\n    return (\\n        <div>\\n            <ul>\\n              {items.map((item, index) => (\\n                  <li key={index}>{item}</li>\\n              ))}\\n            </ul>\\n            <button onClick={addItem}>Add an item</button>\\n        </div>\\n    );\\n}"],[2022,"To create a component in React it is enough to write a function or a class that returns JSX. Functional components are the standard nowadays, and they can be created with a simple function that takes `props` and returns markup.","A component in React can be created in two ways: as a functional component or as a class. In practice functional components are used more often, since they are simpler and support hooks, which let you work with state and effects.\\n\\nAn example of a simple functional component:\\n\\nYou can also use class components, but they are encountered more rarely in modern projects:","function Greeting(props) {\\n   return <h1>Hello, {props.name}!</h1>;\\n}\\n\\nclass Greeting extends React.Component {\\n   render() {\\n       return <h1>Hello, {this.props.name}!</h1>;\\n   }\\n}"],[2023,"Browsers cannot read JSX directly, since it is not valid JavaScript. For the browser to be able to execute it, JSX has to be transpiled (converted) into ordinary JavaScript. This is normally done by means of tools such as Babel, which turn JSX into function calls the browser understands.","JSX (JavaScript XML) is a syntax extension to JavaScript that lets you write markup right in your code, which makes creating components in React easier. Browsers cannot interpret JSX directly, however, since it is not standard JavaScript. For the browser to be able to read and execute it, JSX has to be turned into ordinary JavaScript. Babel — a popular transpilation tool — is normally used for this.\\n\\nHere is an example of the conversion:\\n\\nThis JSX code will be turned into JavaScript by Babel:","const element = <h1>Hello, world!</h1>;\\n\\nconst element = React.createElement(\'h1\', null, \'Hello, world!\');"],[2024,"Keys are unique identifiers that React uses in order to keep track of elements when rendering lists. They help React update only the elements that have changed efficiently rather than re-rendering the whole list. Keys must be unique among sibling elements.","When React renders a list of elements, it uses \\"keys\\" in order to optimize the update process. Keys help React identify which elements have been changed, added or removed, and that lets it update only the parts of the DOM that really have changed.\\n\\nAn example of using keys in a list:\\n\\nHere `item.id` is used as the unique key for each item of the list. This matters, because without keys React will not be able to update the elements correctly when the list changes, which can lead to the application behaving incorrectly.\\n\\nKeys only have to be unique among the elements at the same level. If there is no unique identifier, as happens with an array of data, you can use the array element\'s index, although that is not always recommended, since the indexes can change when the list is updated.\\n\\nIndexes can be used when the list will be constant and the items will not be deleted or changed (by CRUD actions, for example)","const listItems = items.map((item) =>\\n   <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>\\n);"],[2025,"Array indexes can be used as keys if the order of the items does not change and the items themselves have no unique identifiers. This can lead to problems, however, if the order of the items changes or if items are added to or removed from the list dynamically.","Using array indexes as keys can be acceptable in situations where the list is static or where the items do not change their order. For small or immutable lists, for example, indexes can be used as keys. In dynamic lists, however, where items can be added or removed, using indexes can lead to incorrect behaviour. This is because keys are what help React tell the elements apart.\\n\\nIf React uses indexes, it may mistakenly conclude that an element has not changed, when in fact its order or its content has.","const items = [\'Apple\', \'Banana\', \'Orange\'];\\nconst listItems = items.map((item, index) => <li key={index}>{item}</li>);"],[2026,"Class components use the class syntax and have methods for working with state and with the lifecycle. Functional components are simple functions that return JSX. Functional components used not to be able to have state, but with the arrival of hooks in React they gained that ability. Functional components are now used more often, thanks to their simpler syntax and their flexibility.","Class components were the main way of creating components with state and lifecycle methods before hooks were introduced. They use classes and methods such as `render()`, `componentDidMount()` and `componentDidUpdate()` in order to manage state and side effects. For example:\\n\\nWith the arrival of hooks, functional components became preferable, since their structure is simpler. Hooks such as `useState` and `useEffect` let functional components manage state and the lifecycle. For example:\\n\\nFunctional components are more readable, easier to test and give better performance thanks to optimizations such as `useMemo` and `useCallback`.","class MyComponent extends React.Component {\\n    constructor(props) {\\n          super(props);    \\n          this.state = { count: 0 };  \\n    }  render() {\\n          return <div>{this.state.count}</div>;  \\n    } \\n}\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n    const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n    \\n    useEffect(() => {\\n          document.title = `You clicked ${count} times`;  \\n    }, [count]);  \\n  \\n    return <div>{count}</div>; \\n}"],[2027,"Conditional rendering is a way of displaying elements in React on the basis of particular conditions. It works just like conditional statements in JavaScript. You can show one component if a condition is true and another if it is false, for example.","Conditional rendering in React lets you change which elements or components are displayed in the interface dynamically, according to the current state or props. The simplest way of implementing it is to use an `if` statement or the ternary operator. For example:\\n\\nThe ternary operator can make the code more compact:\\n\\nYou can also use a logical `&&` in order to render a component if a condition is true:\\n\\nConditional rendering is the main way of building dynamic user interfaces, where different components or elements appear according to the application\'s state.","function Greeting({ isLoggedIn }) {\\n    if (isLoggedIn) {\\n          return <h1>Welcome back!</h1>;  \\n    } else {\\n          return <h1>Please sign up.</h1>;  \\n    } \\n}\\n\\nfunction Greeting({ isLoggedIn }) {\\n    return isLoggedIn ? <h1>Welcome back!</h1> : <h1>Please sign up.</h1>; \\n}\\n\\nfunction ShowWarning({ showWarning }) {\\n    return (\\n          <div>      \\n             {showWarning && <p>Warning: Something went wrong!</p>}    \\n          </div>  \\n  ); \\n}"],[2028,"An HOC (higher-order component) is a function that takes a component and returns a new component. This lets you reuse logic between different components without changing their source code. HOCs are useful when you need to add shared functionality such as authorization or state management.","Higher-order components (HOCs) are one of React\'s patterns for reusing logic between components. An HOC is a function that takes a component as its argument and returns a new component, wrapping it in additional logic. An HOC can add authorization functionality, for example:\\n\\nHere `withAuth` adds the authorization logic without changing the component itself. That lets you use the same HOC for different components, adding shared functionality. HOCs are often applied to tasks such as caching, authorization, state management and data processing.\\n\\nIt is worth noting that with the arrival of hooks, many of the tasks solved by means of HOCs can be implemented through custom hooks, which are often simpler and more convenient. HOCs nevertheless remain an important part of the React ecosystem and are widely used in large applications.","function withAuth(Component) {\\n    return function AuthenticatedComponent(props) {\\n          const isAuthenticated = /* the authorization-check logic */;    \\n          if (!isAuthenticated) {\\n                return <Redirect to=\\"/login\\" />;    \\n          }    \\n          return <Component {...props} />;  \\n    }; \\n}"],[2029,"Pure components prevent re-renders automatically if their props or state have not changed. This is done by means of a shallow comparison of the values. Ordinary components, on the other hand, render every time their parent component updates, even if their props or state have not changed.","In React, pure components are components that perform a shallow comparison of their props and state in order to avoid unnecessary re-renders. This improves performance, especially in large applications. If a component\'s props have not changed, for example, a pure component will not re-render:\\n\\nOrdinary components, such as `React.Component`, do not perform that comparison and will re-render every time their parent component renders, even if the props have not changed. Pure components are useful when components render the same data over and over, since they prevent unnecessary updates. They should be used with care, however, since a shallow comparison does not account for nested objects and arrays, which can lead to incorrect behaviour.","class MyComponent extends React.PureComponent {\\n    render() {\\n          return <div>{this.props.value}</div>;  \\n    } \\n}"],[2030,"Render props are a pattern in React in which a component receives a function for rendering through its props. This approach lets you pass dynamic logic and data between components, which gives flexibility in rendering.","The render-props pattern lets components pass their rendering flexibly through a function given to them as a prop. This is useful when you need to pass dynamic logic or state down to child components. For example:\\n\\nA description:\\n\\n- MouseTracker is a component that tracks the mouse\'s coordinates and passes them into a function through the `render` prop.\\n- Instead of a class component, a functional component with the `useState` hook is used in order to hold the mouse\'s position.\\n- `handleMouseMove` updates the `x` and `y` coordinates as the mouse moves.\\n- In the `App` component a function is passed through the `render` prop that determines how the data is to be displayed — in this case, showing the mouse\'s position on the screen.\\n\\nThe functional approach with hooks therefore lets you implement the render-props pattern just as easily, keeping both the logic and the flexibility.","import React, { useState } from \'react\'; \\n\\nfunction MouseTracker({ render }) {\\n    const [position, setPosition] = useState({ x: 0, y: 0 });  \\n\\n    const handleMouseMove = (event) => {\\n          setPosition({ x: event.clientX, y: event.clientY });  \\n    };  \\n  \\n    return (\\n          <div style={{ height: \'100vh\' }} onMouseMove={handleMouseMove}>      \\n            {render(position)}    \\n          </div>  \\n    ); \\n} \\n\\nfunction App() {\\n    return (\\n          <MouseTracker render={({ x, y }) => (\\n              <h1>Mouse position: {x}, {y}</h1>    \\n          )}/>  \\n    ); \\n} \\nexport default App;"],[2031,"Component lifecycles in React are methods that let you control a component\'s behaviour at the various stages of its \\"life\\". The main stages are mounting (the component is created), updating (the component changes) and unmounting (the component is removed).","A component\'s lifecycle in React consists of several stages: mounting, updating and unmounting. These stages include methods that let you control the component at each of them.\\n- Mounting: it happens when the component is first added to the DOM.\\n- `componentDidMount()` — it is called immediately after the component has been inserted into the DOM. This method is often used to perform asynchronous operations such as data requests.\\n\\n- Updating: the component re-renders when its props or state change.\\n- `componentDidUpdate(prevProps, prevState)` — it is called immediately after an update. It is used to perform actions based on the new props or state.\\n\\n- Unmounting: it happens when the component is removed from the DOM.\\n- `componentWillUnmount()` — it is called before the component is removed. This method is useful for cleaning up — cancelling timers or stopping requests, for example.\\n\\nWith functional components and hooks, the lifecycle methods are replaced by hooks such as `useEffect`, which lets you handle effects on mounting, updating and unmounting:\\n\\nThe lifecycles are important to understand, since they help you manage state, asynchronous requests and the clean-up of resources in React components properly.","useEffect(() => {\\n   // the code executed on mounting\\n\\n   return () => {\\n       // the code executed on unmounting\\n   };\\n}, []);"],[2032,"To cancel a component\'s update in React you can use the shouldComponentUpdate method in class components or React.memo for functional components. These approaches let you determine whether the component should re-render according to the changes in its props or state.","React has several ways of preventing a component from re-rendering. For class components you can override the `shouldComponentUpdate` method, which takes the new props and state. If the method returns `false`, the component will not update:\\n\\nFor functional components you can use `React.memo`, which wraps the component and prevents it from re-rendering if its props have not changed:\\n\\nYou can also use `useMemo` and `useCallback` in order to optimize computations and functions inside a component. It is important to remember, however, that cancelling a re-render is not always the right thing to do, since it can complicate the code and lead to bugs if the changes are not tracked properly.","class MyComponent extends React.Component {\\n   shouldComponentUpdate(nextProps, nextState) {\\n       return nextProps.value !== this.props.value;\\n   }\\n\\n   render() {\\n       return <div>{this.props.value}</div>;\\n   }\\n}\\n\\nconst MyComponent = React.memo(({ value }) => {\\n   return <div>{value}</div>;\\n});"],[2033,"Code splitting is a technique that lets you break an application down into small parts which are loaded as they are needed. Lazy loading loads those parts only when they really are needed, which helps reduce the size of the initial download and improves the speed of the application.","Code splitting and lazy loading in React let you optimize an application\'s loading and its performance. Code splitting divides an application into separate chunks that can be loaded as they are needed. In React the `React.lazy` function and the `Suspense` component are often used to implement this technique.\\n\\nAn example of its use:\\n\\nIn this example the `LazyComponent` component will be loaded only when it is needed, and while it is loading the user is shown a loading indicator. Lazy loading helps cut down the time needed for the application\'s initial load, especially if it is large and has a lot of dependencies.\\n\\nThese techniques are important to building scalable, performant applications, since they let you load only the parts of the code you need, which improves the user experience considerably.","import React, { Suspense, lazy } from \'react\';\\n\\nconst LazyComponent = lazy(() => import(\'./LazyComponent\'));\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n   return (\\n       <div>\\n           <h1>My App</h1>\\n           <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\\n               <LazyComponent />\\n           </Suspense>\\n       </div>\\n   );\\n}"],[2034,"Refs in React are used to gain access to DOM elements or to the instances of class components. They let you control elements directly — to focus an input or to animate something, for example — bypassing React\'s usual data flow.","Refs in React provide a way of gaining access to DOM elements or to the instances of class components. They are created by means of `React.createRef()` or the `useRef()` hook in functional components.\\n\\nAn example of using refs with a class component:\\n\\nIn this example `inputRef` is used to gain access to the input and focus it when the button is clicked.\\n\\nIn functional components this is done by means of `useRef`:\\n\\nRefs are useful for performing DOM manipulations such as managing focus, running animations or integrating with libraries that require access to the elements. They should be used with care, however, since they can break the principles of React\'s unidirectional data flow.","class MyComponent extends React.Component {\\n   constructor(props) {\\n       super(props);\\n       this.inputRef = React.createRef();\\n   }\\n\\n   focusInput = () => {\\n       this.inputRef.current.focus();\\n   };\\n\\n   render() {\\n       return (\\n           <div>\\n               <input ref={this.inputRef} type=\\"text\\" />\\n               <button onClick={this.focusInput}>Focus Input</button>\\n           </div>\\n       );\\n   }\\n}\\n\\nimport React, { useRef } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n   const inputRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n   const focusInput = () => {\\n       inputRef.current.focus();\\n   };\\n\\n   return (\\n       <div>\\n           <input ref={inputRef} type=\\"text\\" />\\n           <button onClick={focusInput}>Focus Input</button>\\n       </div>\\n   );\\n}"],[2035,"In React events are handled by means of attributes called \\"event handlers\\". To handle a click on a button, for example, we can pass a function into the `onClick` attribute. It is important to use arrow-function syntax or to bind your methods, in order to access the `this` context correctly.","In React events are handled by means of attributes called event handlers. Every JSX element can have handlers of its own — `onClick`, `onChange` and so on. Event handlers are passed as functions.\\n\\nHere is a simple example in which a click event on a button is handled:\\n\\nIn this example, when the user clicks the button, the `handleClick` function fires and displays a message.\\n\\nIt is important to remember that in React it is recommended that you use arrow functions or bind your methods, in order to work with the `this` context correctly. Here is an example with a class component:\\n\\nIn this example we bind the `handleClick` method in the constructor, so that the `this` context points to the component\'s instance. Events in React therefore let us build interactive user interfaces that respond to the user\'s actions easily.","import React from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n   const handleClick = () => {\\n       alert(\'Button was clicked!\');\\n   };\\n\\n   return (\\n       <button onClick={handleClick}>Click me</button>\\n   );\\n}\\n\\nclass MyComponent extends React.Component {\\n   constructor(props) {\\n       super(props);\\n       this.handleClick = this.handleClick.bind(this);\\n   }\\n\\n   handleClick() {\\n       alert(\'Button was clicked!\');\\n   }\\n\\n   render() {\\n       return <button onClick={this.handleClick}>Click me</button>;\\n   }\\n}"],[2036,"The `useMemo` hook is used to optimize performance, by remembering the result of a computation and avoiding its being performed again while the dependencies are unchanged. It is not intended, however, for controlling the rendering of components the way `shouldComponentUpdate` does, which is responsible for deciding whether a component needs updating.","The `useMemo` hook in React serves to optimize performance by remembering the result of a computation. It returns the stored value if the dependencies have not changed, which can prevent unnecessary recomputation. `useMemo` does not control whether a component should render, however.\\n\\nFor example, when using `useMemo`:\\n\\nHere `useMemo` will recompute `computedValue` only when `number` changes, which prevents unnecessary computation.\\n\\nIn contrast, `shouldComponentUpdate` in class components determines whether the component should render, on the basis of changes in its props or state. For example:\\n\\nHere the `shouldComponentUpdate` method lets you control the rendering process, which `useMemo` does not. So although both mechanisms serve to optimize, they solve different problems: `useMemo` is for remembering the results of computations, while `shouldComponentUpdate` is for controlling the rendering process","import React, { useMemo } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction ExpensiveComponent({ number }) {\\n   const computedValue = useMemo(() => {\\n       // A long computation\\n       return number * 2;\\n   }, [number]);\\n\\n   return <div>{computedValue}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nclass MyComponent extends React.Component {\\n   shouldComponentUpdate(nextProps, nextState) {\\n       return nextProps.value !== this.props.value;\\n   }\\n\\n   render() {\\n      return <div>{this.props.value}</div>;\\n   }\\n}"],[2037,"`useMemo` and `useCallback` are both intended to optimize performance in React, but they serve different purposes. `useMemo` remembers computed values, while `useCallback` remembers functions. That lets you avoid unnecessary computation and the creation of functions on every render of a component.","The `useMemo` and `useCallback` hooks are used to optimize performance in React functional components, but they solve different problems.\\n\\n`useMemo` remembers the result of a computation and returns the stored value if the dependencies have not changed. This is useful when you have expensive computations that ought not to be repeated unnecessarily:\\n\\n`useCallback` remembers a function, preventing it from being created anew on every render of the component as long as the dependencies stay unchanged. This is especially useful when the function is passed down to child components that may optimize their rendering on the basis of function references:\\n\\nIn this example the `handleClick` function will be created only once and will not be recreated on every render, which can help you avoid unnecessary re-renders of the child components.\\n\\nIt is important to understand that `useMemo` returns a value while `useCallback` returns a function. Both help improve an application\'s performance by preventing unnecessary computation and renders, but they are used in different situations.","import React, { useMemo } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction App({ number }) {\\n   const computedValue = useMemo(() => {\\n       // A long computation\\n       return number * 2;\\n   }, [number]);\\n\\n   return <div>{computedValue}</div>;\\n}\\n\\nimport React, { useCallback } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n   const handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n       console.log(\'Button clicked!\');\\n   }, []);\\n\\n   return <button onClick={handleClick}>Click me</button>;\\n}"],[2038,"A component unmounts in React when it is removed from the DOM. That can happen for various reasons, such as a change in the parent component\'s state or props. On unmounting, the `componentWillUnmount` lifecycle method (in class components) or the clean-up functions from `useEffect` (in functional components) are called, which lets you perform any necessary clean-up.","Unmounting a component in React is the process of removing the component from the virtual DOM and, ultimately, from the real DOM. It can happen for various reasons, such as a change in the parent component\'s state that leads to a change in its rendering, or in the conditional rendering of components.\\n\\nClass components have a special lifecycle method, `componentWillUnmount`, which is called before the component unmounts. This is the place where you can perform clean-up — cancelling subscriptions or clearing timers, for example:\\n\\nIn functional components the `useEffect` hook is used, which can return a clean-up function. That function will be called when the component unmounts:\\n\\nIn this example the timer is cleared when the component unmounts, which prevents a memory leak. The unmounting of components in React is therefore important to managing resources and to ensuring they are used efficiently.","class MyComponent extends React.Component {\\n   componentWillUnmount() {\\n       // We clear the timer\\n       clearTimeout(this.timer);\\n   }\\n\\n   render() {\\n       return <div>Hello!</div>;\\n   }\\n}\\n\\nimport React, { useEffect } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n   useEffect(() => {\\n       const timer = setTimeout(() => {\\n           console.log(\'Timer executed\');\\n       }, 1000);\\n\\n       // The clean-up function\\n       return () => clearTimeout(timer);\\n   }, []);\\n\\n   return <div>Hello!</div>;\\n}"],[2039,"Rendering in React happens in two stages: the first is the virtual render, when React creates the virtual DOM, and the second is the update of the real DOM. When the state or the props change, React computes the differences between the previous and the new virtual DOM and updates only the parts of the real DOM that have changed, which makes rendering efficient.","Rendering in React is the process of turning components and their state into a displayed interface. When a component mounts or updates, React creates a virtual representation (the virtual DOM) of the component and compares it against the previous version.\\n\\nRendering happens in two main stages:\\n\\n- Creating the virtual DOM: when a component renders, React creates a virtual representation that represents its current state. That makes it easier to track changes.\\n- Comparing and updating the real DOM: when a component\'s state or props change, React creates the virtual DOM again and compares it against the previous one. This process is called \\"reactive rendering\\" or \\"diffing\\". React computes the minimal number of changes needed in order to update the real DOM. That lets it avoid unnecessary DOM manipulation, which improves performance considerably.\\n\\nIn this example, when the user clicks the button the `count` state changes. React creates a new virtual DOM with the updated value, compares it against the previous virtual DOM and updates only the text inside the `<p>` element, which makes the rendering efficient.\\n\\nThis rendering process in React gives high performance and responsive user interfaces, letting developers concentrate on their components\' logic rather than on DOM manipulation.","import React, { useState } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n   const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n   return (\\n       <div>\\n           <p>You clicked {count} times</p>\\n           <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Click me</button>\\n       </div>\\n   );\\n}"],[2040,"The asynchronous loading of components in React, or code splitting, lets you load components as they are needed rather than loading the whole of the application\'s code at once. This is achieved by means of dynamic imports and the `React.lazy` function, which lets you reduce the initial bundle size considerably and speed the page\'s loading up.","The asynchronous loading of components, or code splitting, in React lets you load parts of an application only when they are needed. This reduces the initial loading time considerably, since the user does not have to download the whole of the application\'s code at once. Instead they receive only the components needed for the display.\\n\\nIn React, dynamic imports by means of `React.lazy` and `Suspense` are often used to implement code splitting.\\n\\nIn this example `LazyComponent` is loaded asynchronously. When the `App` component renders, `LazyComponent` has not yet been loaded. Instead the `fallback` component is displayed (in this case the text \\"Loading...\\") while `LazyComponent` loads.\\n\\nThe use of code splitting lets you:\\n- Reduce the size of the initial bundle: only the components you need are loaded, which makes the application load faster.\\n- Improve the user experience: users see content sooner, even if some parts of the application are still loading.\\n\\nThe asynchronous loading of components in React is therefore a powerful tool for optimizing performance and improving the interaction with the user.","import React, { Suspense, lazy } from \'react\'; \\n\\nconst LazyComponent = lazy(() => import(\'./LazyComponent\')); \\n\\nfunction App() {\\n    return (\\n          <div>\\n              <h1>Hello World</h1>   \\n              <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>       \\n                <LazyComponent />      \\n              </Suspense>    \\n          </div>  \\n    ); \\n}"],[2041,"Error Boundaries are special components in React that let you catch and handle errors arising in their child components. They prevent an application from being destroyed completely, letting you display a fallback UI instead when an error occurs. Error Boundaries work only for errors arising during rendering, not for errors in event handlers or in asynchronous code.","Error Boundaries in React are a mechanism that lets developers handle errors arising in a component or in its children. This is especially useful for improving the user experience, since it lets you avoid an application being destroyed completely when an error occurs in one of its components.\\n\\nError Boundaries are created by means of class components and require at least one of the lifecycle methods to be implemented: `static getDerivedStateFromError` or `componentDidCatch`.\\n\\nIn this example, if any child component throws an error, the `ErrorBoundary` will catch it and update its state in order to show a fallback interface with the message \\"Something went wrong\\".\\n\\nError Boundaries help you:\\n- Avoid the application crashing: if an error occurs, instead of the interface failing completely, the application can show a fallback UI.\\n- Localize errors: the errors will be handled within a particular component rather than affecting the whole application.\\n- Send information about errors: using the `componentDidCatch` method, you can send information about the errors that have occurred to the server for further analysis.\\n\\nError Boundaries therefore play a key role in building reliable, resilient React applications.","class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {\\n    constructor(props) {\\n          super(props);    \\n          this.state = { hasError: false };  \\n    }  \\n    static getDerivedStateFromError(error) {\\n          // We update the state so that the next render shows the fallback UI    \\n          return { hasError: true };  \\n    }  \\n    componentDidCatch(error, info) {  \\n      // You can send an error report to the server    \\n      console.error(\\"Error:\\", error, info);  \\n    }  \\n    render() {\\n          if (this.state.hasError) {  \\n            // You can display any fallback UI      \\n            return <h1>Something went wrong.</h1>;    \\n          }    \\n          return this.props.children;  \\n    } \\n}"],[2042,"A React Fragment is a component that lets you group several elements without creating an additional node in the DOM. This is useful when you need to return several elements from a component while avoiding the addition of unnecessary wrappers, which can affect the styles and the semantics of the HTML.","A React Fragment is a mechanism in React that lets you combine several elements into one group without adding unnecessary nodes to the real DOM. This is especially useful when you want to return several elements from a component but do not want to wrap them in an unnecessary element such as a `<div>`, which can complicate the HTML structure and cause potential problems with your CSS styles.\\n\\nYou can use a Fragment in several ways:\\n- By means of the `<React.Fragment>` component:\\n\\n- Or by means of the shorthand `<>` and `</>`:\\n\\nIn both cases the result will be that the heading and the paragraph are returned without an unnecessary element in the DOM. This matters, since it lets you keep the HTML structure clean, improves the readability of the code and makes styling easier.\\n\\nA React Fragment can also take keys, which is useful in cases where you use it in lists:\\n\\nIn this example each item of the list is wrapped in a Fragment with a key, which helps React track the changes in the list properly.\\n\\nThe use of a React Fragment helps you avoid redundancy in your markup and improves performance, which makes it an important tool when developing React applications.","import React from \'react\'; \\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n    return ( \\n         <React.Fragment>   \\n            <h1>A heading</h1>      \\n            <p>A description.</p>    \\n         </React.Fragment>  \\n    ); \\n}\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n    return (\\n          <>    \\n            <h1>A heading</h1>   \\n            <p>A description.</p>   \\n          </>  \\n    ); \\n}\\n\\nfunction MyList({ items }) {\\n    return (\\n          <>   \\n             {items.map(item => (     \\n                  <React.Fragment key={item.id}>      \\n                      <h2>{item.title}</h2>     \\n                      <p>{item.description}</p>        \\n                  </React.Fragment>      \\n             ))}    \\n          </>  \\n    ); \\n}"],[2043,"Prop drilling is the process of passing data through several levels of components when the intermediate components do not use that data. It can complicate the structure of the components and make managing an application\'s state harder, since every change requires all of the intermediary components to be updated.","Prop drilling in React is the practice of passing props through several levels of components. It happens when data or state is passed from a parent component down to a child through intermediate components that do not use that data themselves.\\n\\nIf you have a component `A` that holds some state, for example, and you want to pass that state into component `C`, which is a child of `A`, but the intermediate component is `B`, you will have to pass the props through `B`, even though `B` does not use that data:\\n\\nIn this example `data` is passed from `A` to `C` through `B`, even though `B` does not use that state. This leads to difficulties in managing the state and complicates the code, since any change of state in `A` requires every intermediate component to be updated.\\n\\nTo avoid problems with prop drilling, developers can use various approaches:\\n- The Context API: it lets you pass data through the component tree without having to pass it through every wrapper.\\n- State in the parent component: if the state is needed for only one component, you can consider holding it in the parent component.\\n- State managers: the use of libraries such as Redux or MobX, which let you manage state at a higher level and avoid passing props.\\n\\nProp drilling is therefore an important concept in React that is worth taking into account when designing an application\'s architecture, since it can affect both the readability of your code and how comfortable it is to work with.","function A() {\\n    const [data, setData] = useState(\'Hello\');  \\n    return <B data={data} />; \\n} \\n\\nfunction B({ data }) {\\n    return <C data={data} />; \\n} \\n\\nfunction C({ data }) {\\n    return <h1>{data}</h1>; \\n}"],[2044,"In React state is managed by means of hooks such as `useState` for functional components and `this.state` for class ones. State lets components hold and update the data that affects what is displayed. The `useEffect` hook is also used to manage the side effects related to state.","Managing state in React is one of the key aspects of developing interactive user interfaces. A component\'s state is an object containing the data that affects its rendering and its behaviour. When the state changes, React re-renders the component automatically in order to display the new data.\\n\\nIn functional components the `useState` hook is used to manage state. With it you can create a piece of state and a function for updating it:\\n\\nIn this example `count` is the state and `setCount` is the function for updating it. Every time the user clicks the button, the count increases by 1 and the component re-renders with the new value.\\n\\nIn class components state is managed through `this.state` and the `setState` method:\\n\\nHere the `setState` method updates the state, and React calls `render` automatically in order to update the interface.\\n\\nIn addition to `useState`, React offers `useEffect` for managing the side effects related to state, such as API requests, subscriptions and DOM manipulation. That lets you keep a component\'s state in sync with external data.\\n\\nManaging state in React therefore lets you build dynamic, interactive applications, letting components respond to the user\'s actions and change their behaviour.","import React, { useState } from \'react\'; \\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n    const [count, setCount] = useState(0); // We initialize the state with zero  \\n    return (\\n          <div>    \\n            <p>The current count: {count}</p>      \\n            <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>    \\n          </div>  \\n    ); \\n}\\n\\nclass Counter extends React.Component {\\n    constructor(props) {\\n          super(props);    \\n          this.state = { count: 0 };  \\n    }  \\n    increment = () => {\\n          this.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 });  \\n    };  \\n    render() {\\n          return (   \\n             <div>     \\n                  <p>The current count: {this.state.count}</p>        \\n                  <button onClick={this.increment}>Increment</button>      \\n             </div>    \\n          );  \\n    } \\n}"],[2045,"In React four kinds of controlled and uncontrolled entity can be distinguished:\\n- A controlled component (by its parent) — the parent manages the child\'s state through `props`.\\n- An uncontrolled component (by its parent) — the component manages its own state itself (through `useState` or `useRef`).\\n- A controlled form element — the element\'s value is held in `state`, and changes are tracked through `onChange`.\\n- An uncontrolled form element — the value is held directly in the DOM (usually through a `ref`).","1. A controlled component (by its parent)\\nThe parent component manages the child\'s state by means of `props`.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe characteristics:\\n- `InputField` holds no state of its own.\\n- The state is controlled entirely by the parent.\\n\\n2. An uncontrolled component (by its parent)\\nThe component manages its own state itself, without the parent taking part.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe characteristics:\\n- The state (`inputValue`) is held inside the component.\\n- The parent does not control this `input`.\\n- The component is entirely self-contained.\\n\\n3. A controlled form element\\nThe element\'s value is held in `state` and updated through `onChange`.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe characteristics:\\n- React holds the current value (`value`) in `state`.\\n- Every change fires `onChange`, which updates the `state`.\\n- React controls the field\'s contents entirely.\\n\\n4. An uncontrolled form element\\nThe element\'s value is held in the DOM rather than in `state`.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe characteristics:\\n- React does not hold the field\'s value.\\n- The value is accessed directly through a `ref`.\\n- This approach is simpler, but it is not always suitable for complex forms.\\n\\nIn summary\\n\\n- Components (at the parent\'s level):\\n- Controlled → the state is passed through `props`.\\n- Uncontrolled → the component manages its own state itself.\\n\\n- Form elements:\\n- Controlled → React holds the value in `state`.\\n- Uncontrolled → the value lives in the DOM, accessed through a `ref`.","function InputField({ value, onChange }) {\\n  return <input type=\\"text\\" value={value} onChange={onChange} />;\\n}\\n\\nfunction ParentComponent() {\\n  const [inputValue, setInputValue] = useState(\'\');\\n\\n  return (\\n    <InputField\\n      value={inputValue}\\n      onChange={(e) => setInputValue(e.target.value)}\\n    />\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction UncontrolledComponent() {\\n  const [inputValue, setInputValue] = useState(\'\');\\n\\n  const handleSubmit = () => {\\n    alert(\'Input value: \' + inputValue);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input \\n        type=\\"text\\" \\n        value={inputValue} \\n        onChange={(e) => setInputValue(e.target.value)} \\n      />\\n      <button onClick={handleSubmit}>Submit</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction ControlledInput() {\\n  const [value, setValue] = useState(\'\');\\n\\n  const handleChange = (event) => {\\n    setValue(event.target.value);\\n  };\\n\\n  return <input type=\\"text\\" value={value} onChange={handleChange} />;\\n}\\n\\nfunction UncontrolledInput() {\\n  const inputRef = useRef();\\n\\n  const handleSubmit = () => {\\n    alert(\'Input value: \' + inputRef.current.value);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input type=\\"text\\" ref={inputRef} />\\n      <button onClick={handleSubmit}>Submit</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2046,"The Context API is a way of passing data through the component tree without having to pass it through props at every level. To create global state you create a context by means of `React.createContext()`, wrap your components in a provider, and then use `useContext` to access the data in any child component.","The Context API in React provides a way of managing global state and passing data through the component tree, avoiding the need to pass it through every level by means of props. This is especially useful in large applications, where the same state or data has to be available in several components at different levels of the hierarchy.\\n\\nTo create global state using the Context API, follow these steps:\\n- Create the context:\\n\\n- Create a provider in order to manage the state:\\n\\nHere `GlobalProvider` holds the state and the functions for updating it and provides them through `GlobalContext.Provider`.\\n- Use the provider in your application:\\n\\n- Access the context\'s data in the child components:\\n\\nIn this example `ComponentA` uses `useContext` in order to access the global state and the `updateUser` function. When the user clicks the button the state is updated, and every component subscribed to this context will be re-rendered with the new data.\\n\\nThe Context API makes managing global state easier by removing the need for prop drilling","import React, { createContext, useState } from \'react\'; \\nconst GlobalContext = createContext(); // We create the context\\n\\nconst GlobalProvider = ({ children }) => {\\n    const [state, setState] = useState({ user: null });  \\n  \\n    const updateUser = (user) => {\\n          setState({ user }); // The function for updating the state  \\n    };  \\n  \\n    return (  \\n        <GlobalContext.Provider value={{ state, updateUser }}>    \\n            {children}    \\n        </GlobalContext.Provider>  \\n    ); \\n};\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n    return (  \\n        <GlobalProvider>    \\n            <ComponentA />    \\n        </GlobalProvider>  \\n      ); \\n}\\n\\nimport React, { useContext } from \'react\'; \\n\\nfunction ComponentA() {\\n     const { state, updateUser } = useContext(GlobalContext);\\n     // We use useContext to access the state  \\n   \\n     return (  \\n         <div>   \\n            <p>The user: {state.user ? state.user.name : \'No data\'}</p>      \\n            <button onClick={() => updateUser({ name: \'Ivan\' })}>Update the user</button>    \\n         </div>  \\n     ); \\n}"],[2047,"The `useEffect` hook is used to perform side effects in functional components, such as API requests, subscriptions or DOM manipulation. It takes two arguments: a callback function and an array of dependencies. The array determines when the effect is to run — on the first render, for example, or when particular variables change. To clean effects up you can return a function from the hook.","The `useEffect` hook is a powerful tool for managing side effects in React functional components. It lets you perform actions after the component has been painted, which makes it ideal for handling tasks such as:\\n- API requests: fetching data when the component mounts.\\n- Subscriptions: setting up subscriptions to events such as changes in data.\\n- DOM manipulation: changing elements on the page after rendering.\\n\\nAn example of using `useEffect` to fetch data from an API:\\n\\nIn this example `useEffect` runs only once — when the component mounts — thanks to the empty array of dependencies. That lets you avoid unnecessary requests on every update.\\n\\nTo manage subscriptions you can use the clean-up function that is returned from `useEffect`:\\n\\nThe array of dependencies is an important aspect of `useEffect`. If you pass variables into the array, `useEffect` will run every time those variables change. That lets you control the effect\'s behaviour according to your application\'s state.\\n\\n`useEffect` therefore gives developers a flexible way of working with side effects, helping keep functional components clean and predictable.","import React, { useEffect, useState } from \'react\'; \\n\\nfunction DataFetchingComponent() {\\n    const [data, setData] = useState([]);  \\n  \\n    useEffect(() => {\\n          fetch(\'https://api.example.com/data\')      \\n            .then(response => response.json())      \\n            .then(data => setData(data))      \\n            .catch(error => console.error(\'Error:\', error));  \\n     }, []); \\n     // An empty array of dependencies means the effect runs only on the first render  \\n      \\n     return (\\n           <ul>   \\n              {data.map(item => (    \\n                    <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>      \\n              ))}    \\n           </ul>  \\n     ); \\n}\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n    const subscription = someAPI.subscribe(data => {\\n          setData(data);  \\n    });  \\n    return () => {\\n          subscription.unsubscribe(); // We clean the subscription up when the component unmounts  \\n    }; \\n}, []);"],[2048,"`useEffect` runs asynchronously after rendering, which lets you update the DOM without blocking the display. `useLayoutEffect`, by contrast, runs synchronously immediately after the DOM has changed and before it is displayed, which lets you take measurements or manipulate elements. That can be useful when you need to react to changes at once and avoid the interface flickering.","Both hooks, `useEffect` and `useLayoutEffect`, serve to perform side effects in functional components, but there are important differences between them in when and how they run.\\n\\nuseEffect: this hook runs after the component has rendered and the DOM has been updated. It runs asynchronously, which means it does not block the rendering process. That makes it ideal for performing API requests, subscriptions and other operations that do not require an immediate effect on the DOM.\\n\\nAn example of using `useEffect`:\\n\\nuseLayoutEffect: this hook runs synchronously immediately after the DOM has changed, but before the browser paints. That lets you take measurements and manipulate the DOM while avoiding flicker or incorrectly painted elements. It will block the display until every effect has finished running, which can have a negative effect on performance if it is used incorrectly.\\n\\nAn example of using `useLayoutEffect`:\\n\\nIn this example `useLayoutEffect` guarantees that the measurement will be taken before the paint, which is useful when you need to update the component\'s state on the basis of those measurements.\\n\\nThe choice between `useEffect` and `useLayoutEffect` depends on the situation. If you need to perform an action that does not require the interface to be updated immediately, use `useEffect`. If direct interaction with the DOM is needed — to measure the size or position of elements, for example — use `useLayoutEffect`.","useEffect(() => {\\n    console.log(\'The component was painted or updated\'); \\n}, [dependency]);\\n\\nuseLayoutEffect(() => {\\n    const height = ref.current.clientHeight; // We measure the element\'s height  \\n    console.log(\'The height:\', height); \\n}, []);"],[2049,"Custom hooks are functions that let you combine state logic and effects so that they can be reused in various components. They begin with the prefix `use`, can use other hooks inside them, and return values or functions for managing state. Custom hooks help you cut down code duplication and make an application easier to maintain.","Custom hooks in React are a powerful tool for creating reusable logic that can be used in various components. Custom hooks let you wrap state logic and effects in a single function, which makes code easier to maintain and to test.\\n\\nTo create a custom hook, follow these steps:\\n- Define a function whose name begins with `use`:\\n\\nIn this example `useFetch` is a custom hook that fetches data from the given URL and manages the loading and error state.\\n- Use the custom hook in a component:\\n\\nIn this `DataDisplay` component we use the `useFetch` custom hook in order to fetch the data. That lets us avoid duplicating code and makes the component cleaner and more readable.\\n\\nCustom hooks can be used for any state logic or side effects, which makes them a very versatile tool in React. By using custom hooks you can organize and structure your code, making it easier to maintain and to test.","import { useState, useEffect } from \'react\'; \\n\\nfunction useFetch(url) {\\n    const [data, setData] = useState(null);  \\n    const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);  \\n    const [error, setError] = useState(null);  \\n  \\n    useEffect(() => {\\n          fetch(url)      \\n            .then(response => {    \\n                  if (!response.ok) {       \\n                       throw new Error(\'A network error\');        \\n                  }        \\n                  return response.json();      \\n             })      \\n             .then(data => {    \\n                   setData(data);        \\n                   setLoading(false);      \\n             })      \\n             .catch(error => {    \\n                   setError(error);        \\n                   setLoading(false);      \\n             });  \\n     }, [url]);  \\n   \\n     return { data, loading, error }; // We return the values and the state \\n}\\n\\nimport React from \'react\'; \\nimport { useFetch } from \'./useFetch\'; // We import the custom hook \\n\\nfunction DataDisplay({ url }) {\\n    const { data, loading, error } = useFetch(url); // We use the hook  \\n  \\n    if (loading) return <p>Loading...</p>;  \\n    if (error) return <p>Error: {error.message}</p>;  \\n  \\n    return (\\n          <ul>   \\n             {data.map(item => (   \\n                    <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>      \\n             ))}    \\n         </ul>  \\n    ); \\n}"],[2050,"The `useRef` hook is used to gain access to DOM elements in React. You can create a ref by means of `useRef` and then assign it to an element through the `ref` attribute. That lets you manage focus using methods such as `focus()` to set focus on an element — when the component loads, for example, or on an event such as a button click.","The `useRef` hook in React provides a way of interacting with DOM elements, letting you keep a reference to an element between renders without re-rendering the component. This is especially useful for managing focus on elements, which matters for improving accessibility and the interaction with the user.\\n\\nAn example of using `useRef` to manage focus on a text field:\\n\\nIn this example we create a ref by means of `useRef` and then assign it to the text field by means of the `ref` attribute. When the `Set focus` button is clicked, the `handleFocus` function is called, which sets focus on the text field.\\n\\nYou can use `useRef` to manage focus not only on text fields but on other elements as well, such as buttons or containers. That makes `useRef` a powerful tool for building interactive, accessible interfaces.","import React, { useRef } from \'react\'; \\n\\nfunction FocusInput() {\\n    const inputRef = useRef(null); // We create the ref  \\n  \\n    const handleFocus = () => {\\n          inputRef.current.focus(); // We set focus on the element  \\n    };  \\n  \\n    return (\\n          <div>    \\n            <input ref={inputRef} type=\\"text\\" placeholder=\\"Enter some text\\" />      \\n            <button onClick={handleFocus}>Set focus</button>    \\n          </div>  \\n    ); \\n}"],[2051,"The `useImperativeHandle` hook lets you control which methods and properties are available to a parent component through a ref. It is used in combination with `forwardRef` in order to create components that can expose controlled interfaces, letting parents call particular functions on their children — focusing or animating, for example.","The `useImperativeHandle` hook in React lets you customize the instance that will be returned when refs are used on components. This is useful when you want a parent component to be able to call particular methods on a child component while preserving encapsulation and avoiding access to the internal data\\n\\nUsing `useImperativeHandle` requires the component to use `React.forwardRef`, in order to ensure the ref is passed correctly. Here is an example of its use:\\n\\nIn this example `CustomInput` uses `useImperativeHandle` in order to expose the `focus` and `clear` methods, which can be called from the `ParentComponent` parent component. That lets the parent manage the child component\'s state while keeping the internal logic encapsulated.\\n\\n`useImperativeHandle` is therefore a powerful tool for creating components that can expose controlled interfaces, which is especially useful in large applications with complex component hierarchies.","import React, { useImperativeHandle, forwardRef, useRef } from \'react\'; \\n\\nconst CustomInput = forwardRef((props, ref) => {\\n    const inputRef = useRef();  \\n  \\n    useImperativeHandle(ref, () => ({\\n          focus: () => {   \\n             inputRef.current.focus(); // We expose a method for focusing    \\n          },    \\n          clear: () => {   \\n            inputRef.current.value = \'\'; // We expose a method for clearing the field    \\n          }  \\n    }));  \\n  \\n    return <input ref={inputRef} type=\\"text\\" />; \\n}); \\n\\nfunction ParentComponent() {\\n    const inputRef = useRef();  \\n  \\n    const handleFocus = () => {\\n          inputRef.current.focus(); // We call the focus method on the child component  \\n    };  \\n    const handleClear = () => {\\n          inputRef.current.clear(); // We call the clear method on the child component  \\n    };  \\n  \\n    return (\\n          <div>   \\n             <CustomInput ref={inputRef} />      \\n             <button onClick={handleFocus}>Set focus</button>      \\n             <button onClick={handleClear}>Clear the field</button>    \\n          </div>  \\n    ); \\n}"],[2052,"`React.memo` is a higher-order function that lets you memoize components, preventing them from re-rendering if their props have not changed. This can improve performance, especially in large applications with a great many components. Use `React.memo` when you have components that take immutable props and do not depend on global state.","`React.memo` is a function built into React that lets you optimize functional components by preventing them from rendering unnecessarily. When you wrap a component in `React.memo`, React will compare the component\'s props, and if they have not changed the component will not re-render, which helps reduce the load on the application\'s performance.\\n\\nHere is an example of using `React.memo`:\\n\\nIn this example `ExpensiveComponent` will re-render only if the `data` prop changes. When the user clicks the button to increment the counter, `ExpensiveComponent` will not re-render, since its props have not changed.\\n\\nIt is important to note that `React.memo` works only with functional components and has no effect on class components. If you need to optimize class components, you can use the `shouldComponentUpdate` method or `PureComponent`.\\n\\nIt is also worth bearing in mind that `React.memo` does not always lead to an improvement in performance. If a component often receives new props, or if the memoization adds extra cost for the comparison, it can slow the application down. `React.memo` should therefore be used deliberately, in cases where you can achieve a real benefit.\\n\\nIn summary, `React.memo` is a useful tool for optimizing the performance of React applications, especially when it comes to components that take immutable data.","import React from \'react\'; \\n\\nconst ExpensiveComponent = React.memo(({ data }) => {\\n    console.log(\'Rendering ExpensiveComponent\');  \\n    return <div>{data}</div>; \\n}); \\n\\nfunction App() { \\n  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);  \\n  const data = \\"Some data\\";  \\n  \\n  return (\\n      <div>   \\n             <ExpensiveComponent data={data} />      \\n             <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment the counter: {count}</button>   \\n      </div> \\n  ); \\n}"],[2053,"Optimizing the performance of React applications can involve various approaches, such as using `React.memo` to memoize components, applying the `useCallback` and `useMemo` hooks in order to prevent unnecessary re-renders, and introducing the lazy loading of components by means of `React.lazy`. It is also important to keep an eye on the application\'s state and to avoid excessive renders, which will help reduce response time and improve overall performance.","Optimizing the performance of React applications can be achieved by a great many different methods, all aimed at reducing rendering time and improving the interface\'s responsiveness.\\n- Using `React.memo`: this method lets you memoize functional components. The component will not re-render if its props have not changed. This is especially useful for components that update often but receive the same data.\\n\\n- The `useCallback` and `useMemo` hooks: these hooks let you memoize functions and values in order to avoid unnecessary re-renders. `useCallback` is used to memoize functions and `useMemo` to memoize values. This helps prevent unnecessary re-renders of child components.\\n\\n- The lazy loading of components: by means of `React.lazy` and `Suspense` you can load components only when they are needed, which reduces the initial bundle size and the loading time.\\n\\n- Avoiding excessive renders: try to use local state where you can, and avoid passing large objects as props. Split your components up and use rendering optimizations in order to minimize the number of re-renders.\\n\\nAll of these methods will help you build a more performant application, one able to handle the user\'s requests more efficiently and more quickly.","import React from \'react\';\\n \\nconst MyComponent = React.memo(({ title }) => {\\n    console.log(\'Rendering MyComponent\');  \\n    return <h1>{title}</h1>; \\n});\\n\\nimport React, { useState, useCallback } from \'react\'; \\n\\nfunction ParentComponent() {\\n    const [count, setCount] = useState(0);  \\n  \\n    const increment = useCallback(() => {\\n          setCount(count + 1);  \\n    }, [count]); \\n  \\n    return <button onClick={increment}>Increment: {count}</button>; \\n}\\n\\nimport React, { Suspense } from \'react\'; \\n\\nconst LazyComponent = React.lazy(() => import(\'./LazyComponent\')); \\n\\nfunction App() {\\n    return (\\n          <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>    \\n            <LazyComponent />    \\n          </Suspense>  \\n    ); \\n}"],[2054,"Memoization is an optimization technique that stores the results of functions on the basis of their arguments, in order to avoid repeating the computation. In React memoization is often done by means of the `useMemo` and `useCallback` hooks, which help you store the results of computations and functions, preventing unnecessary renders of components. This is especially useful in cases where a function performs a heavy computation or returns a new object on every render.","Memoization is an optimization method that lets you store the results of function calls and return the cached results if the same arguments are used again. This can improve performance considerably, especially for functions with heavy computations or ones that return new objects on every call.\\n\\nIn React memoization is done by means of the `useMemo` and `useCallback` hooks:\\n- `useMemo`: this hook is used to memoize computed values. It takes a function and an array of dependencies, and will recompute the value only when the dependencies change.\\n\\n- `useCallback`: this hook is used to memoize functions. It works like `useMemo`, but it returns a memoized version of the function.\\n\\nThe use of memoization helps prevent unnecessary re-renders of components and improves the interface\'s responsiveness. Memoization does require resources in order to store the cached values, however, so its use should be justified, especially for simple, fast computations.","import React, { useMemo } from \'react\'; \\n\\nfunction Component({ items }) {\\n    const sortedItems = useMemo(() => {\\n          return items.sort(); // A heavy operation  \\n    }, [items]);  \\n  \\n    return <ul>{sortedItems.map(item => <li key={item}>{item}</li>)}</ul>; \\n}\\n\\nimport React, { useState, useCallback } from \'react\'; \\n\\nfunction ParentComponent() {\\n    const [count, setCount] = useState(0); \\n   \\n    const increment = useCallback(() => {\\n          setCount(c => c + 1);  \\n   }, []);  \\n  \\n    return <button onClick={increment}>Increment: {count}</button>; \\n}"],[2055,"To prevent unnecessary renders of components in React you can use methods such as memoizing components by means of `React.memo`, optimizing functions by means of the `useCallback` and `useMemo` hooks, and applying `shouldComponentUpdate` or `PureComponent` for class components. It is also worth avoiding passing new objects and functions as props, since that can cause child components to re-render.","Preventing unnecessary renders of components in React is the key to building performant, responsive applications. The main techniques that will help you with this:\\n- Using `React.memo`: this method lets you memoize functional components, which prevents them from re-rendering if their props have not changed.\\n\\n- Optimizing functions by means of hooks: the `useCallback` and `useMemo` hooks let you memoize functions and values in order to avoid unnecessary re-renders.\\n\\n- Using `shouldComponentUpdate` and `PureComponent`: in class components you can use the `shouldComponentUpdate` method in order to control when the component should re-render. You can also inherit from `PureComponent`, which performs a shallow comparison of the props and the state automatically.\\n- Avoid passing new objects and functions: if you pass new objects or functions as props, that will cause a re-render. Try to use memoization or to define your functions outside the render.\\n- Splitting components up: split your components into smaller ones where you can. That will let React manage the rendering process better and not re-render the whole component hierarchy when that is not required.\\n\\nKeeping to these practices will help you improve your applications\' performance considerably and make them more responsive for your users.","import React from \'react\'; \\n\\nconst MyComponent = React.memo(({ title }) => {\\n    console.log(\'Rendering MyComponent\');  \\n     return <h1>{title}</h1>; \\n});\\n\\nimport React, { useState, useCallback } from \'react\'; \\n\\nfunction ParentComponent() {\\n     const [count, setCount] = useState(0);  \\n   \\n     const increment = useCallback(() => {\\n           setCount(c => c + 1);  \\n     }, []);  \\n   \\n     return <button onClick={increment}>Increment: {count}</button>; \\n}"],[2056,"The Profiler API is a tool in React that helps you measure the performance of components by showing how long they take to render. You can use the `<Profiler>` component to wrap parts of your application and track the rendering time and the reasons an update happened. This is useful for finding and eliminating performance bottlenecks.","The Profiler API in React is a powerful tool that lets developers track their applications\' performance. It helps you determine which components spend a lot of time rendering and where performance can be improved.\\n\\nTo use the Profiler API you have to wrap the components you want to profile in a `<Profiler>` component. The Profiler component takes two main props:\\n\\nid\\nA unique identifier for the profile in question. This can be useful for telling different profiled components in your application apart.\\n\\nonRender\\nA callback function that will be called after every render. It takes the following parameters:\\n- `id`: the component\'s identifier.\\n- `phase`: the phase in which the render happened (\\"mount\\" or \\"update\\").\\n- `actualDuration`: the time spent rendering the component, in milliseconds.\\n- `baseDuration`: the estimated rendering time if no changes had happened.\\n- `startTime`: the time the render started.\\n- `commitTime`: the time the render finished.\\n\\nAn example of using the Profiler API:\\n\\nBy using the Profiler API you can analyse your application\'s performance, determine which components need optimizing, and make decisions about how to improve the overall performance.","import React, { Profiler } from \'react\'; \\n\\nfunction onRender(id, phase, actualDuration, baseDuration, startTime, commitTime) {\\n    console.log(`The component ${id} rendered in the ${phase} phase in ${actualDuration}ms.`); \\n} \\n\\nfunction App() {\\n    return (\\n          <Profiler id=\\"MyComponent\\" onRender={onRender}>    \\n            <MyComponent />    \\n          </Profiler>  \\n    ); \\n}"],[2057,"react-window and react-virtualized are libraries for virtualizing long lists in React, which let you render only the visible items, improving performance considerably. react-window is simpler and lighter than react-virtualized and is suitable for most cases. To use it you need to create a virtualized list, passing an array of data into it and configuring the height of the items.","Virtualization is a technique that lets you render only the items the user can see, which reduces the load on performance considerably when working with large lists. The `react-window` and `react-virtualized` libraries provide convenient components for this task.\\n\\nreact-window\\nThis library offers a simple implementation of virtualized lists. Its main components include:\\n- `FixedSizeList`: for lists whose items are a fixed size.\\n- `VariableSizeList`: for lists whose items are of variable height.\\n\\nreact-virtualized\\nThis library is more powerful and provides more capabilities than react-window, but it is also more complicated to use. It includes components for virtualizing lists, tables, grids and even maps.\\n\\nUsing these libraries lets you handle large sets of data efficiently while keeping the interface performant and responsive.","import React from \'react\'; \\nimport { FixedSizeList as List } from \'react-window\'; \\n\\nconst items = Array.from({ length: 1000 }, (_, index) => `Item ${index + 1}`); \\nfunction MyList() {\\n    return (\\n          <List    \\n            height={300}    \\n            itemCount={items.length}      \\n            itemSize={35}      \\n            width={300}    \\n          >      \\n            {({ index, style }) => (   \\n                 <div style={style}>{items[index]}</div>     \\n            )}    \\n          </List>  \\n     ); \\n}\\n\\nimport React from \'react\'; \\nimport { List } from \'react-virtualized\'; \\n\\nconst items = Array.from({ length: 1000 }, (_, index) => `Item ${index + 1}`); \\nfunction MyList() {\\n    return (\\n          <List   \\n             width={300}      \\n             height={300}      \\n             rowCount={items.length}      \\n             rowHeight={35}      \\n             rowRenderer={({ index, key, style }) => (   \\n                    <div key={key} style={style}>{items[index]}</div>      \\n             )}    \\n          />  \\n    ); \\n}"],[2058,"Debouncing and throttling are techniques that limit the number of times a function is called, in order to improve an application\'s performance. Debouncing delays a function\'s execution until a series of calls has stopped, whereas throttling lets a function run at a fixed rate. Both methods are useful in situations such as handling user input or scroll events.","Debouncing and throttling are two techniques used to control how often functions are called in JavaScript and React. They are especially useful for handling events that can cause a great many function calls in a short space of time, such as typing text into a field or scrolling the page.\\n\\nDebouncing\\nThis technique cancels a function call if a new event happens within a given time. That means the function will be called only after a series of calls has finished. This is useful in real-time search, for example, where you want to send a request to the server only once the user has finished typing.\\n\\nAn example of implementing debouncing using hooks:\\n\\nThrottling\\nThis technique limits a function to being called once in a given interval of time. That means the function will be called on a regular basis, however often the event happens.\\n\\nAn example of implementing throttling using hooks:\\n\\nBoth techniques help improve performance by controlling how often functions are called and avoiding an excessive load on the application.","import React, { useState, useEffect } from \'react\'; \\n\\nfunction DebounceExample() {\\n    const [searchTerm, setSearchTerm] = useState(\'\');  \\n    const [debouncedTerm, setDebouncedTerm] = useState(searchTerm);  \\n  \\n    useEffect(() => {\\n          const handler = setTimeout(() => {  \\n              setDebouncedTerm(searchTerm);    \\n          }, 300);    \\n      \\n          return () => {   \\n             clearTimeout(handler);    \\n          };  \\n    }, [searchTerm]);  \\n  \\n    return (\\n          <div>   \\n             <input     \\n                  type=\\"text\\"        \\n                   value={searchTerm}        \\n                  onChange={(e) => setSearchTerm(e.target.value)}      \\n             />      \\n             <p>The debounced term: {debouncedTerm}</p>    \\n          </div>  \\n    ); \\n}\\n\\nimport React, { useState, useEffect } from \'react\'; \\n\\nfunction ThrottleExample() {\\n    const [count, setCount] = useState(0);  \\n  \\n    useEffect(() => {\\n          const handleScroll = () => {\\n               setCount(prevCount => prevCount + 1);    \\n          };    \\n      \\n          const throttledHandleScroll = throttle(handleScroll, 1000);    \\n      \\n          window.addEventListener(\'scroll\', throttledHandleScroll);    \\n          return () => {   \\n             window.removeEventListener(\'scroll\', throttledHandleScroll);    \\n          };  \\n     }, []);  \\n   \\n     return <p>The number of scrolls: {count}</p>; \\n} \\n\\nfunction throttle(func, limit) {\\n    let lastFunc;  \\n    let lastRan;  \\n  \\n    return function() {\\n          const context = this;    \\n          const args = arguments;    \\n          if (!lastRan) {    \\n            func.apply(context, args);      \\n            lastRan = Date.now();    \\n          } else {   \\n             clearTimeout(lastFunc);      \\n             lastFunc = setTimeout(() => {    \\n                   if (Date.now() - lastRan >= limit) {     \\n                        func.apply(context, args);          \\n                        lastRan = Date.now();        \\n                   }      \\n             }, limit - (Date.now() - lastRan));    \\n          }  \\n    }; \\n}"],[2059,"To optimize working with large lists of data you can use several methods: virtualization, pagination and lazy loading. Virtualization (by means of the `react-window` or `react-virtualized` libraries, for example) lets you render only the visible items of a list, which reduces the load on performance. Pagination divides a list into pages, loading only a limited amount of data at a time, while lazy loading loads the data as it is needed — as the user scrolls, for example.","Optimizing work with large lists of data in React applications is an important task, since it directly affects the performance and responsiveness of the interface. There are several effective strategies for achieving it:\\n\\nVirtualization\\nThis method lets you render only the items that lie in the visible area of the screen, which reduces the number of DOM elements considerably and improves performance. Libraries such as `react-window` and `react-virtualized` make implementing virtualized lists easier. With `react-window`, for example, you can create a list that will render only the visible items:\\n\\nPagination\\nThis approach lets you divide a large list into several pages, loading only a limited amount of data at a time. That reduces the load on the client and improves the user experience, since users can load the data as they need it. An example of pagination can be implemented using state and \\"Next\\" and \\"Previous\\" buttons:\\n\\nLazy loading\\nThis approach involves loading the data as it is needed. As the page scrolls, for example, additional items of the list can be loaded. This helps save resources and loading time.\\n\\nBy using these strategies you can improve your application\'s performance considerably and make the interaction with large lists of data smoother and more responsive.","import React from \'react\'; \\nimport { FixedSizeList as List } from \'react-window\'; \\n\\nconst items = Array.from({ length: 1000 }, (_, index) => `Item ${index + 1}`); \\n\\nfunction MyList() {\\n    return ( \\n         <List    \\n            height={300}      \\n            itemCount={items.length}      \\n            itemSize={35}      \\n            width={300}    \\n         >      \\n           {({ index, style }) => (    \\n                 <div style={style}>{items[index]}</div>      \\n           )}    \\n         </List>  \\n     ); \\n}\\n\\nfunction PaginatedList({ items, itemsPerPage }) {\\n    const [currentPage, setCurrentPage] = useState(0);  \\n    const startIndex = currentPage * itemsPerPage;  \\n    const currentItems = items.slice(startIndex, startIndex + itemsPerPage);  \\n  \\n    return (\\n          <div>   \\n             {currentItems.map(item => (    \\n                   <div key={item}>{item}</div>      \\n             ))}      \\n             <button onClick={() => setCurrentPage(prev => Math.max(prev - 1, 0))}>Previous</button>      \\n             <button onClick={() => setCurrentPage(prev => prev + 1)}>Next</button>    \\n          </div>  \\n    ); \\n}"],[2060,"Bundle splitting is a technique that lets you divide your application\'s code into smaller parts that are loaded as they are needed. It reduces the loading time of the initial bundle and improves performance. In React you can use `React.lazy()` and `Suspense` to load components dynamically, and tools such as Webpack to configure bundle splitting.","Bundle splitting is a practice that lets you optimize the loading of web applications by reducing the size of the initial JavaScript bundle. This is especially useful for large applications, where loading all of the code at once can take a long time.\\n\\nThe advantages of bundle splitting:\\n\\n- A reduction in the loading time of the initial page.\\n- The dynamic loading of code needed only at particular moments, which lets you improve the application\'s performance.\\n\\nUsing React.lazy() and Suspense: in React you can use `React.lazy()` to load components dynamically. Here is how it works:\\n\\nIn this example the `LazyComponent` component will be loaded only when it is needed. `Suspense` is used to display a loading indicator while the component loads.\\n\\nConfiguring bundle splitting with Webpack: if you use Webpack, you can configure rules for splitting bundles automatically. By means of the `splitChunks` setting, for example, you can specify how the code is to be split:\\n\\nThis will let Webpack split the code into smaller bundles automatically, which will be loaded as they are needed.\\n\\nIn summary, bundle splitting is a powerful tool for optimizing the performance of your React applications, letting you load code more efficiently and improving the user experience.","import React, { Suspense, lazy } from \'react\'; \\n\\nconst LazyComponent = lazy(() => import(\'./LazyComponent\')); \\n\\nfunction App() {\\n    return ( \\n         <div>    \\n             <h1>The home page</h1>      \\n             <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>     \\n                <LazyComponent />     \\n             </Suspense>    \\n         </div>  \\n    ); \\n}\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n    optimization: {  \\n        splitChunks: {   \\n             chunks: \'all\',    \\n        },  \\n    }, \\n};"],[2061,"React components can be tested by means of libraries such as Jest and React Testing Library. Jest provides the means to write tests, while React Testing Library lets you interact with components the way a user does. The tests can include checks on rendering, on interaction with components and on props.","Testing React components is an important process that helps you make sure your components work correctly and meet the requirements. The main tools that can be used for testing React applications include Jest and React Testing Library.\\n- Jest: this is a JavaScript testing framework that lets you write tests for the various parts of your application. It supports various kinds of test, including unit tests, integration tests and snapshots.\\n- React Testing Library: this library is oriented towards testing components, providing convenient methods for rendering components and interacting with them the way a user does. Here is how you can test a simple component using these tools:\\n\\nIn this example we created a `Greeting` component that takes `name` as a prop and renders a greeting message. We then wrote a test that renders the component and checks that the message is displayed correctly.\\n\\nYou can create different kinds of test for your components:\\n- Unit tests: checking individual functions or components.\\n- Integration tests: checking the interaction between several components.\\n- Snapshot tests: comparing a component\'s rendering against the previous result.\\n\\nTesting components properly helps you find bugs at the early stages of development, which guarantees that the application will run stably and meet the users\' expectations.","// The component \\nconst Greeting = ({ name }) => {\\n    return <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>; \\n}; \\n\\n// The test \\nimport React from \'react\'; \\nimport { render, screen } from \'@testing-library/react\'; \\nimport Greeting from \'./Greeting\'; \\n\\ntest(\'checks that the Greeting component renders with the right name\', () => {\\n    render(<Greeting name=\\"Alex\\" />);  \\n    const greetingElement = screen.getByText(/hello, alex/i);  \\n    expect(greetingElement).toBeInTheDocument(); \\n});"],[2062,"React Testing Library is a tool for testing React components, which lets you check how components render and interact with the user. With this library you can render components in your tests and use various methods to find and interact with elements, imitating the user\'s actions. Its main methods include `render`, `screen`, `getByText` and `fireEvent`. This helps you write more reliable, clearer tests.","React Testing Library is a popular tool for testing React components, which helps developers check their applications\' functionality with confidence. This library focuses on how the user interacts with the components rather than on their internal implementation, which makes the tests more reliable and more resilient to change.\\n\\nInstallation and set-up:\\nTo begin with you need to install the library, if you have not already done so:\\n\\nThe main methods:\\n\\n- `render`: a method that renders a component into a virtual DOM. For example:\\n\\n- `screen`: it provides access to the methods for finding elements in the render. Instead of destructuring from `render`, you can use `screen` for more readable tests:\\n\\n- `getByText`: it lets you find elements by their text. There are other methods too, such as `getByRole` and `getByLabelText`, which help you find elements by various criteria.\\n- `fireEvent`: it lets you imitate interaction with elements — clicks, for example:\\n\\nThe tests:\\nTests written using React Testing Library can check how a component is displayed and how it responds to the user\'s actions. They help you make sure the application works as expected.\\n\\nReact Testing Library lets you write clear, effective tests, which improves the quality and reliability of your applications considerably.","npm install --save-dev @testing-library/react\\n\\nimport { render } from \'@testing-library/react\'; \\nimport MyComponent from \'./MyComponent\'; \\n\\ntest(\'the component renders correctly\', () => {\\n    const { getByText } = render(<MyComponent />);  \\n    expect(getByText(\'Hello, world!\')).toBeInTheDocument(); \\n});\\n\\nimport { render, screen } from \'@testing-library/react\'; \\n\\nrender(<MyComponent />); \\nexpect(screen.getByText(\'Hello, world!\')).toBeInTheDocument();\\n\\nimport { fireEvent } from \'@testing-library/react\'; \\n\\nfireEvent.click(screen.getByText(\'A button\')); \\nexpect(screen.getByText(\'The text after the click\')).toBeInTheDocument();\\n\\ntest(\'the text changes on a click\', () => {\\n    render(<MyComponent />);  \\n    const button = screen.getByRole(\'button\');  \\n    fireEvent.click(button);  \\n    expect(screen.getByText(\'The text has changed!\')).toBeInTheDocument(); \\n});"],[2063,"Server-side rendering (SSR) is a method in which HTML pages are generated on the server and sent to the client rather than being created in the browser by means of JavaScript. This lets pages load faster and improves SEO, since search engines can index the content. In React, frameworks such as Next.js can be used to implement SSR, which make the process of setting up and managing server-side rendering easier.","Server-side rendering (SSR) is an approach in which React components are rendered on the server and returned to the client as HTML. This differs from client-side rendering, where all of the JavaScript is downloaded to the client and the rendering happens in the browser. SSR has several key advantages:\\n- Better performance: since the HTML is generated on the server, users can see the content sooner. This is especially useful for the first loads of a page.\\n- SEO optimization: search engines index content that is already present in the HTML better than content created dynamically by means of JavaScript.\\n- A better user experience: the fast loading of pages helps improve how users perceive an application.\\n\\nImplementing SSR by means of Next.js\\nNext.js is a popular React framework that makes the process of setting up server-side rendering easier. Here is an example of how to do it:\\n- Installing Next.js:\\n\\n- Creating a page: Next.js sets the routes up automatically from the folder structure. Let us create the page `pages/index.js`, for example:\\n\\n- Rendering data on the server: you can use the `getServerSideProps` function in order to load data on the server before the page is rendered:\\n\\nIn this example the data is loaded on the server and passed into the component, which is rendered as HTML. That lets users see the content immediately without waiting for the JavaScript to load.\\n\\nSSR is a powerful tool for building modern web applications with good performance and SEO optimization, and using Next.js makes the task considerably easier.","npx create-next-app@latest my-next-app cd my-next-app\\n\\n// pages/index.js \\nimport React from \'react\'; \\n\\nconst HomePage = () => {\\n    return <h1>Welcome to the home page!</h1>; \\n}; \\n\\nexport default HomePage;\\n\\n// pages/index.js \\nexport async function getServerSideProps() {\\n    const res = await fetch(\'https://api.example.com/data\');  \\n    const data = await res.json();  \\n    return {\\n          props: { data },  \\n    }; \\n} \\n\\nconst HomePage = ({ data }) => {\\n    return <h1>{data.title}</h1>; \\n}; \\n\\nexport default HomePage;"],[2064,"Client-side rendering (CSR) happens in the browser, where JavaScript loads and renders the pages after the initial load, whereas server-side rendering (SSR) generates the HTML on the server and sends it to the client. CSR lets you build dynamic interfaces but can slow the initial load down, whereas SSR gives fast loading and better SEO optimization but requires additional resources on the server.","Client-side rendering (CSR) and server-side rendering (SSR) are the two main approaches to generating HTML for web applications.\\n\\nClient-side rendering (CSR)\\nCSR is a method in which all of the JavaScript is downloaded to the client (the browser) and the browser renders the pages. This approach lets you build interactive, dynamic applications. The initial load can be slow, however, since the user may have to wait for all of the JavaScript to download and execute.\\n\\nThe advantages of CSR:\\n\\n- More responsive, more dynamic user interfaces.\\n- A lighter load on the server, since all of the processing is done on the client.\\n\\nThe disadvantages of CSR:\\n\\n- A long time to the first load, which can make the user experience worse.\\n- Problems with SEO, since search engines may not index dynamically loaded content.\\n\\nIn this case the application is rendered in the browser, and the HTML is generated only once the JavaScript has run.\\n\\nServer-side rendering (SSR)\\nSSR is a method in which the HTML is generated on the server and sent to the client. This approach lets users see the content immediately, which improves both performance and SEO.\\n\\nThe advantages of SSR:\\n\\n- Fast page loading and a better perception.\\n- Better SEO optimization, since the content is already present in the HTML.\\n\\nThe disadvantages of SSR:\\n\\n- An increased load on the server, since the rendering is done there.\\n- A more complicated set-up and management of the application\'s state.\\n\\nAn example of SSR using Next.js:\\n\\nIn this example the data is loaded on the server and sent to the client as ready-made HTML.\\n\\nBoth approaches — CSR and SSR — have their advantages and disadvantages. The choice between them depends on your application\'s requirements, on the user experience you want and on the server\'s resources. The two approaches can often be combined, in order to take advantage of each in different parts of an application.","import React from \'react\'; \\n\\nconst App = () => {\\n    return <h1>Hello, world!</h1>; \\n}; \\n\\nexport default App;\\n\\nexport async function getServerSideProps() {\\n    const res = await fetch(\'https://api.example.com/data\');  \\n    const data = await res.json();  \\n  \\n    return {\\n          props: { data },  \\n    }; \\n} \\n\\nconst Page = ({ data }) => {\\n    return <h1>{data.title}</h1>; \\n}; \\n\\nexport default Page;"],[2065,"Internationalization in React applications lets you adapt an interface for different languages and cultures. To set i18n up you can use the `react-i18next` library, which provides tools for managing language resources and translations. Installing the library involves configuring the languages, the translations and the components that will display the translated strings according to the chosen language.","Internationalization (i18n) is the process of adapting an application to different languages and cultural peculiarities, which lets users interact with the application in their own language. In React applications this process can be made easier by means of the `react-i18next` library, which integrates with `i18next`, a popular internationalization library.\\n- Installation: to begin with you need to install the necessary packages:\\n\\n- Configuring i18next: create a configuration file in order to set i18next up and add your translations. Create `i18n.js`, for example:\\n\\n- Using it in the application: include the configuration in your application\'s root component in order to initialize the internationalization:\\n\\n- Displaying the translated strings: you can now use the `useTranslation` hook in order to get the translated strings in your components:\\n\\n- Changing the language: so that users can change the language, add a function for changing the current one:\\n\\nAs a result your React application will support internationalization, letting users choose a language and providing the translated strings according to the language chosen. This improves the user experience and makes the application more accessible to a wide audience.","npm install react-i18next i18next\\n\\nimport i18n from \'i18next\'; \\nimport { initReactI18next } from \'react-i18next\'; \\n\\ni18n\\n    .use(initReactI18next)  \\n    .init({ \\n         resources: {   \\n            en: {     \\n                 translation: {    \\n                      welcome: \\"Welcome\\",        \\n                 },      \\n            },      \\n              de: {     \\n                 translation: {     \\n                        welcome: \\"Willkommen\\",        \\n                 },      \\n            },    \\n         },    \\n         lng: \\"en\\", // the default language    \\n         fallbackLng: \\"en\\", // the language used if no translation is found    \\n         interpolation: {    \\n             escapeValue: false, // no escaping is needed for React    \\n         },  \\n  }); \\n  \\nexport default i18n;\\n\\nimport React from \'react\'; \\nimport ReactDOM from \'react-dom\'; \\nimport App from \'./App\'; \\nimport \'./i18n\'; // import the configuration \\n\\nReactDOM.render(<App />, document.getElementById(\'root\'));\\n\\nimport React from \'react\'; \\nimport { useTranslation } from \'react-i18next\'; \\n\\nconst Welcome = () => {\\n    const { t } = useTranslation();  \\n  \\n    return <h1>{t(\'welcome\')}</h1>; \\n}; \\n\\nexport default Welcome;\\n\\nconst changeLanguage = (lng) => {\\n    i18n.changeLanguage(lng); \\n};"],[2066,"When working with React it is important to observe several best practices, in order to improve the quality of the code and the application\'s performance. They include: using components in order to reuse code, managing state by means of hooks or context, avoiding unnecessary re-renders by means of `React.memo`, and optimizing performance by means of `useMemo` and `useCallback`. It is also important to follow the principles of accessibility and to write clean, clear code.","Observing best practices in React development not only helps you build reliable applications but also makes your code more readable and more maintainable. Here are several key practices worth bearing in mind:\\n- Use components: break your application down into reusable components. That lets you reduce code duplication and makes the code better structured. For example:\\n\\n- Managing state: use hooks (`useState`, `useReducer`) or context (`useContext`) in order to manage state. That lets you avoid \\"drilling\\" props through several levels of components:\\n\\n- Optimizing performance: use `React.memo` in order to memoize components that should not re-render unnecessarily:\\n\\n- Also apply the `useMemo` and `useCallback` hooks in order to optimize performance when working with computations and functions:\\n\\n- Keep an eye on accessibility: make the interface accessible by adding the appropriate attributes and roles to your elements, so that users with disabilities can interact with the application.\\n\\n- Clean code: write clean, clear code. Avoid components and functions that are too large. Follow the KISS (Keep It Simple, Stupid) and DRY (Don\'t Repeat Yourself) principles. Comments should explain the parts of the code that are not obvious.\\n\\nBy following these best practices you will be able to build high-quality React applications that are easy to maintain and to extend.","const Button = ({ onClick, children }) => (  <button onClick={onClick}>{children}</button> );\\n\\nconst [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\nconst MemoizedComponent = React.memo(({ data }) => {  // the render });\\n\\nconst memoizedValue = useMemo(() => computeExpensiveValue(a, b), [a, b]); \\nconst memoizedCallback = useCallback(() => { /* the handling */ }, [dependencies]);\\n\\n<button aria-label=\\"Close\\" onClick={handleClose}>X</button>"],[2067,"Hydration is the process in which statically generated HTML code received from the server is made interactive by means of JavaScript on the client. This process involves \\"attaching\\" React to the already existing HTML, which lets React handle events and interface updates. Hydration matters for improving performance and ensuring pages load quickly when server-side rendering is used.","Hydration is a key concept related to server-side rendering (SSR) in modern web applications. When you use SSR, the server generates the HTML code and sends it to the client. For that HTML to become interactive, however, \\"hydration\\" is required — the process in which JavaScript is attached to the statically generated HTML.\\n\\nHow it works\\nDuring hydration React loads all of the necessary scripts and binds them to the existing HTML elements. That lets React manage the state and the interface\'s updates. When the page loads, for example, the browser receives static HTML code, which is displayed immediately. Then, once the JavaScript has loaded, React \\"hydrates\\" that HTML, letting it become interactive:\\n\\nWhy it is needed\\nHydration matters because it combines the advantages of server-side rendering (a fast initial load and better SEO optimization) with the interactivity that client-side rendering provides. That lets users see the content immediately and then makes it interactive without the page having to be loaded again.\\n\\nProblems and optimization\\nDuring hydration problems can arise if the HTML structure created on the server does not match what React expected on the client. That can lead to warnings and errors. To avoid this it is important to follow the same rules and the same structure when rendering components on the server and on the client.\\n\\nHydration is an important step in building high-performance web applications, letting you make effective use of both the capabilities of server-side rendering and the interactivity of client-side JavaScript.","import React from \'react\'; \\nimport ReactDOM from \'react-dom\'; \\nimport App from \'./App\'; \\n\\nReactDOM.hydrate(<App />, document.getElementById(\'root\'));"],[2068,"Batching in React is the process of combining several state updates into one, in order to avoid unnecessary re-renders of components. When several `setState` calls happen in the same event or function, React can combine them and perform the updates all at once, which makes rendering more efficient and faster.","Batching is an important concept in React that lets you optimize an application\'s performance by reducing the number of times components are re-rendered. When you update a component\'s state by means of the `setState` function, React does not render the component immediately. Instead it accumulates (batches) the state changes and applies them all at the same time.\\n\\nHow batching works\\nConsider the following code, for example:\\n\\nHere, when you click the button, both state updates are called (`setCount` and `setName`). Instead of rendering the component twice — once for each update — React combines them and renders the whole component only once. That reduces the number of rendering operations considerably and improves the application\'s performance.\\n\\nWhen batching does not work\\nIt is important to note that batching works only inside React events. If you update the state outside a React event — in an asynchronous function or in a `setTimeout`, for example — every state update will cause a separate render:\\n\\nIn this case React will render the component twice, since the calls happen outside the context of its events.\\n\\nThe advantages of batching\\nBatching helps improve an application\'s performance, especially in large components with a great deal of state. It also contributes to a smoother user experience, since users see the interface\'s updates sooner and more smoothly.\\n\\nUnderstanding batching in React therefore lets developers optimize their applications and write more efficient code.","const MyComponent = () => {\\n    const [count, setCount] = useState(0);  \\n    const [name, setName] = useState(\'Alice\');  \\n\\n    const handleClick = () => {\\n          setCount(count + 1);    \\n          setName(\'Bob\');  \\n    };  \\n  \\n    return <button onClick={handleClick}>Click me</button>; \\n};\\n\\nconst handleAsyncUpdate = () => {\\n    setCount(count + 1);  \\n    setName(\'Bob\');\\n}; \\n  \\nsetTimeout(handleAsyncUpdate, 1000);"],[2069,"In React a component updates when its state or its props change. That can happen when `setState` is called, when the parent component passes new props, or when React detects a change in the context. Components can also update when lifecycle methods such as `componentDidUpdate` are used.","Components update in React for several reasons, and understanding these mechanisms will help you write more efficient, more performant code. Here are the main cases in which a component updates:\\n\\nA change of state\\nWhen you call `setState`, React re-renders the component in order to display the new state. For example:\\n\\nHere, every time the button is clicked, `count` is updated and the component renders again with the new value.\\n\\nA change of props\\nComponents also update when their props change. If the parent component passes new prop values, the child components will update automatically. For example:\\n\\nIf `value` in the parent changes, `Child` will receive the new value automatically.\\n\\nA change of context\\nIf a component is subscribed to changes in a context by means of `useContext`, it will be updated when the context\'s value changes.\\n\\nLifecycle methods\\nIn class components updates can also be initiated by means of lifecycle methods such as `componentDidUpdate`, which is called after the component has updated.\\n\\nForcing an update\\nYou can also force a component to update by means of the `forceUpdate` method, although this should be used very carefully, since it can lead to the application behaving sub-optimally.\\n\\nUnderstanding when and why components update helps developers optimize their applications\' performance by avoiding unnecessary re-renders and keeping the interface responsive.","const MyComponent = () => {\\n    const [count, setCount] = useState(0);  \\n  \\n    return ( \\n         <div>    \\n             <p>{count}</p>      \\n             <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>    \\n         </div>  \\n    ); \\n};\\n\\nconst Parent = () => {\\n    const [value, setValue] = useState(\'Hello\');  \\n  \\n    return <Child message={value} />; \\n}; \\n\\nconst Child = ({ message }) => <p>{message}</p>;"],[2070,"React Reconciliation is the process by which React updates the interface, determining what changes need to be made to the virtual DOM and how to reflect them in the real DOM. The process involves comparing the old and the new virtual DOM, which lets React update only the components that have changed efficiently rather than repainting the whole application.","React Reconciliation is a key concept that determines how React manages updates to the interface. When a component\'s state or props change, React starts the reconciliation process in order to determine the changes needed in the interface.\\n\\nThe virtual DOM\\nReact uses a virtual DOM — a lightweight copy of the real DOM — in order to track changes in the interface. When an update happens, React creates a new virtual DOM and compares it against the previous one.\\n\\nComparison (diffing)\\nDuring reconciliation React uses the \\"Reconciliation\\" algorithm in order to determine which parts of the virtual DOM have changed. This algorithm is optimized for finding the differences between the old and the new virtual DOM quickly. If you have a list of items, for example, and you change one of them, React will check exactly which items have changed and update only those:\\n\\nIf one of the list\'s items changes, React will update only that item.\\n\\nUpdating the real DOM\\nOnce React has determined the changes, it applies them to the real DOM. This process minimizes the number of operations on the real DOM, which speeds rendering up considerably and improves the application\'s performance.\\n\\nKeys\\nTo make the reconciliation process easier, it is important to use unique keys for lists of items. That helps React identify which items have changed, been added or been removed more quickly:\\n\\nUnderstanding the reconciliation process in React helps developers optimize rendering and improve their applications\' performance, which leads to a more responsive user interface.","const MyList = ({ items }) => (\\n    <ul>  \\n        {items.map(item => (   \\n             <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>    \\n        ))}  \\n    </ul> \\n);\\n\\n{items.map(item => (\\n    <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li> \\n))}"],[2071,"A portal in React is a mechanism that lets you render child components in a container that lies outside the parent component\'s hierarchy. This is useful for creating elements such as modal windows or tooltips, which have to be independent of the other components and unaffected by their styles or their position.","Portals in React let you render child components in a different place in the DOM tree from their parent component. This can be especially useful for components that need particular placement in the interface, such as modal windows, pop-up menus or notifications.\\n\\nCreating a portal\\nTo create a portal you can use the `ReactDOM.createPortal` method. This method takes two arguments: the element to be rendered and the container you want to put it in. Here is an example:\\n\\nWhere to use portals\\nPortals are often used to create modal windows, which have to cover the whole screen but must not affect the styles or the behaviour of the other components. Since a portal is rendered in the container you specify, you can easily control its styles, its position and its behaviour independently of the parent components.\\n\\nThe advantages\\nThe use of portals makes managing elements such as modal windows and notifications easier, giving greater flexibility in their placement and their styling. It also lets you avoid the problems with overlapping styles that can arise when you try to embed modal windows directly in the component hierarchy.\\n\\nUnderstanding portals in React helps developers manage the display of elements effectively without disturbing the application\'s structure.","import ReactDOM from \'react-dom\'; \\n\\nconst MyModal = ({ children, onClose }) => {\\n    return ReactDOM.createPortal(\\n          <div className=\\"modal\\">    \\n            <button onClick={onClose}>Close</button>      \\n            {children}    \\n          </div>,    \\n          document.getElementById(\'modal-root\') // the container in index.html  \\n     ); \\n};"],[2072,"Reactivity is an approach to programming in which the interface updates automatically when the data or the state changes. In React it is implemented through the system of state and props, which lets developers manage the changes in an application easily and keep the data and the display consistent.","Reactivity is the concept that lets an application\'s interface respond to changes in the data or the state automatically, without the interface having to be updated by hand. In the context of React this is achieved by means of the system of state and props, which governs when and how components are to be re-rendered.\\n\\nHow reactivity works in React\\nEvery component in React can have state of its own, which is managed by means of hooks such as `useState`. When a component\'s state changes (through a call to `setState`, for example), React initiates a re-render of the component and of all of its children automatically:\\n\\nIn this example, when the user clicks the button, `count` increases and React updates the display automatically.\\n\\nThe advantages of reactivity\\nReactivity makes developing interfaces easier, since developers can concentrate on managing the data and its changes without worrying about how those changes will be reflected in the interface. It also lets you build more responsive, more dynamic applications that can adapt to changes in the data quickly.\\n\\nReactivity in other libraries\\nAlthough reactivity has become a synonym for React, this principle is also used in other libraries and frameworks such as Vue.js and Angular. They apply similar concepts in order to update the interface automatically, which is convenient and makes the development process easier.\\n\\nUnderstanding reactivity matters for building modern web applications, since it underlies many of the methods of managing state and updating an interface.","const Counter = () => {\\n    const [count, setCount] = useState(0);  \\n  \\n    return ( \\n         <div>     \\n            <p>{count}</p>      \\n            <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>    \\n         </div>  \\n    ); \\n};"],[2073,"Diffing is the process in which React compares the old and the new representation of the virtual DOM in order to determine what changes need to be made to the real DOM. Patching is the stage at which React makes those changes, updating only the elements that have changed rather than repainting the whole component tree.","Diffing and patching are important processes in React that help optimize updates to the interface and improve applications\' performance. They let React manage changes in the virtual DOM efficiently and minimize the number of operations on the real DOM.\\n\\nDiffing\\nWhen a component\'s state or props change, React creates a new representation of the virtual DOM and compares it against the previous one. This process lets React determine which elements have been changed, added or removed. React uses an optimized comparison algorithm, which works on the principle that:\\n- If two elements are of different types, React removes the old element and adds a new one.\\n\\n- If an element has the same key, React updates its properties.\\n\\n- If the keys differ, React removes the old element and creates a new one.\\n\\nPatching\\nOnce the comparison process has finished, React moves on to patching, where it makes the changes to the real DOM. This process involves minimizing the operations, so as to update only the parts of the interface that really have changed. If only one value in a list has changed, for example, React will update only that element:\\n\\nIf the list changes and only one element is updated, React will apply the changes efficiently to that element alone.\\n\\nThe advantages\\nThe use of the comparison and patching algorithm helps React avoid unnecessary operations on the real DOM, which can be expensive in time terms. This gives a smoother user interface and improves the application\'s overall performance.\\n\\nUnderstanding the comparison and patching process matters for developing high-performance React applications, since it lets developers control and optimize updates to the interface better.","const List = ({ items }) => (\\n    <ul>   \\n        {items.map(item => (    \\n            <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>    \\n        ))}  \\n    </ul> \\n);"],[2074,"Reactivity in React works through the use of state and hooks such as `useState` and `useEffect`. When the state changes, React initiates a re-render of the components that depend on that state automatically, which keeps the data and the interface consistent. Reactivity is achieved through a mechanism of subscribing to changes in the state, together with the virtual DOM, which minimizes updates to the real DOM.","To implement reactivity in plain JavaScript you can use several of the language\'s key mechanisms. One popular approach is the use of getters and setters in combination with the \\"Observer\\" pattern. Let us look at how this can be done at an advanced level.\\n\\nStep 1: creating a reactive object\\nTo begin with, let us create a reactive object using `Proxy`. `Proxy` lets you intercept the basic operations (reading a property, assigning a value and so on) and define custom behaviour for them.\\n\\nStep 2: implementing the \\"Observer\\" pattern\\nTo notify the subscribers of changes, let us implement a simple observer system.\\n\\nStep 3: connecting the reactivity and the observers\\nNow that we have a reactive object and an observer system, we can connect them. For that we modify the `handler` in the `reactive` function, so as to use `Dep` for tracking the dependencies and notifying the subscribers.\\n\\nStep 4: using the reactive state\\nWe can now use our reactive system to track changes automatically and update the DOM or perform other actions.","function reactive(target) {\\n    const handler = {\\n        get(target, property, receiver) {\\n            // Logic for tracking dependencies can go here\\n            console.log(`Reading ${property}`);\\n            return Reflect.get(target, property, receiver);\\n        },\\n        set(target, property, value, receiver) {\\n            // Logic for notifying the observers can go here\\n            console.log(`Setting ${property}: ${value}`);\\n            return Reflect.set(target, property, value, receiver);\\n        }\\n    };\\n\\n    return new Proxy(target, handler);\\n}\\n\\nconst state = reactive({ count: 0 });\\n\\nclass Dep {\\n    constructor() {\\n        this.subscribers = new Set();\\n    }\\n\\n    depend() {\\n        if (activeUpdate) {\\n            // We register the active update as a dependency\\n            this.subscribers.add(activeUpdate);\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    notify() {\\n        // We notify every subscriber of the changes\\n        this.subscribers.forEach(sub => sub());\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nlet activeUpdate = null;\\n\\nfunction autorun(update) {\\n    function wrappedUpdate() {\\n        activeUpdate = wrappedUpdate;\\n        update();\\n        activeUpdate = null;\\n    }\\n\\n    wrappedUpdate();\\n}\\n\\nfunction reactive(target) {\\n    const depsMap = new Map();\\n\\n    const handler = {\\n        get(target, property, receiver) {\\n            let dep = depsMap.get(property);\\n            if (!dep) {\\n              dep = new Dep();\\n              depsMap.set(property, dep);\\n            }\\n\\n            dep.depend(); // We register the dependency\\n\\n            return Reflect.get(target, property, receiver);\\n        },\\n        set(target, property, value, receiver) {\\n            const result = Reflect.set(target, property, value, receiver);\\n            let dep = depsMap.get(property);\\n            if (dep) {\\n              dep.notify(); // We notify the subscribers\\n            }\\n            return result;\\n        }\\n    };\\n\\n    return new Proxy(target, handler);\\n}\\n\\nconst state = reactive({ count: 0 });\\n\\nautorun(() => {\\n    console.log(`The counter: ${state.count}`);\\n});\\n\\nstate.count++; // It will log \\"The counter: 1\\" automatically"],[2075,"Suspense in React is a component that lets you handle asynchronous operations such as loading data or components and display a fallback UI until the data is ready. This helps improve the user experience, letting developers manage the loading state and keep the interface smooth.","Suspense is a powerful feature in React intended for managing the asynchronous loading of data and components. It lets you display a fallback UI (loading indicators, for example) until the loading of the data or components you need has finished.\\n\\nHow Suspense works\\nWhen a component wrapped in Suspense starts loading data asynchronously, React temporarily replaces it with the fallback UI, which can be configured by means of the `fallback` property. Once the data has loaded, the component renders with the new data automatically.\\n\\nAn example of using Suspense to load data by means of `React.lazy`:\\n\\nIn this example the text \\"Loading...\\" is displayed while `LazyComponent` loads.\\n\\nWhy use Suspense\\nSuspense lets developers manage the user interface while waiting for asynchronous operations. This improves how an application is perceived, since users see loading indicators instead of being faced with a blank screen or a frozen interface.\\n\\nAsynchronous components and data\\nSuspense can be used not only to load components but also to manage the loading of data through asynchronous functions. In the future React plans to improve Suspense\'s support for managing the asynchronous loading of data, making this approach more universal.\\n\\nAn example with data: imagine you have a component that loads data from an API. Using Suspense, you can manage the loading state:\\n\\nSuspense provides a powerful tool for managing asynchronous operations in React, giving a smoother, more responsive user interface. Understanding how to use it will help developers improve their applications\' UX.","const LazyComponent = React.lazy(() => import(\'./LazyComponent\')); \\n\\nconst App = () => (\\n    <React.Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>  \\n        <LazyComponent />  \\n    </React.Suspense> \\n);\\n\\nconst fetchData = () => {\\n    return new Promise((resolve) => {  \\n        setTimeout(() => resolve(\'Data loaded\'), 2000);  \\n    }); \\n}; \\n\\nconst DataComponent = () => {\\n    const data = fetchData(); // An asynchronous call  \\n  \\n    return <div>{data}</div>; \\n}; \\n\\nconst App = () => (\\n    <React.Suspense fallback={<div>Loading data...</div>}>   \\n         <DataComponent />  \\n    </React.Suspense> \\n);"],[2076,"CSS-in-JS libraries let you write styles right in your JavaScript code. In `styled-components` styles are created as components, which makes it easier to change styles dynamically according to state and to use themes.","CSS-in-JS libraries such as `styled-components` provide the ability to write components\' styles in JavaScript, which lets you create dynamic, styled components. An example of using `styled-components` in React involves importing the library and creating a component with styles:\\n\\nThis makes creating and managing dynamic styles easier, since the styles can be changed according to the component\'s state or the props it was given. The approach also supports themes, which makes it useful for building responsive, scalable interfaces.","import styled from \'styled-components\';\\nconst Button = styled.button`\\n  background: blue;\\n  color: white;\\n  padding: 10px 20px;\\n  border-radius: 5px;\\n  &:hover {\\n      background: darkblue;\\n  }\\n`;\\nexport default Button;"],[2077,"The event loop is JavaScript\'s mechanism for handling asynchronous tasks. In React the event loop is used to handle events such as clicks or data requests. When changes happen in the data or in a component\'s state, React responds by putting the updates in a queue and following the event loop in order to process them without blocking the main thread.","The event loop in JavaScript is responsible for the asynchronous execution of code and for handling callbacks, timers and events. React makes active use of the event loop in order to manage the rendering of components and to handle changes in state or props.\\n\\nWhen you change the state in React (through `setState`, for example), that does not cause the UI to update immediately. Instead the change is put in a queue, and React waits for the current execution of the code to finish before updating the component during the next cycle of the event loop.\\n\\nReact therefore manages asynchronous tasks efficiently, minimizing unnecessary re-renders and improving performance.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nWhen you click the button, `setCount` changes the state, and that is put in the queue for the event loop\'s next update. React will wait for the current tasks to finish and then update the UI.","function Example() {\\n    const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n    useEffect(() => {\\n        console.log(\\"Component rendered\\", count);\\n    });\\n\\n    return (\\n        <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>\\n            Increment\\n        </button>\\n    );\\n}"],[2078,"- In class components the state is held in `this.state` and changed through `this.setState()`.\\n- In functional ones (with hooks) the state is created through `useState()` and updated by calling the setter function.","1. Class components\\nThe state is initialized in the constructor:\\n\\nTo update it, `this.setState()` is used:\\n\\n`setState` can take a function for dependent updates:\\n\\n2. Functional components (with hooks)\\nThe state is declared through `useState`:\\n\\nUpdating it is done by calling the setter function (`setCount`):\\n\\nFor complex updates you can pass a function:","class Counter extends React.Component {\\n    constructor(props) {\\n        super(props);\\n        this.state = { count: 0 };\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nincrement = () => {\\n    this.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 });\\n};\\n\\nthis.setState((prevState) => ({ count: prevState.count + 1 }));\\n\\nimport { useState } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n    const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n}\\n\\nconst increment = () => {\\n    setCount(count + 1);\\n};\\n\\nsetCount(prevCount => prevCount + 1);"],[2079,"The best way is refs. In functional components `useRef` is used, in class ones `React.createRef()`. A ref gives direct access to the DOM node once it has been created.","1. Functional components (hooks)\\nCreating a ref:\\n\\nBinding it to an element through `ref`:\\n\\nAccessing the DOM:\\n\\n2. Class components\\nCreating a ref:\\n\\nBinding and using it:\\n\\n3. Callback refs (an obsolete way, but useful to know)\\nAn example in class components:\\n\\nWhen to use refs:\\n- Focusing, animations, measuring an element\'s size.\\n- Integration with libraries (`D3.js`, `GreenSock`, for example).\\n\\nThe limitations:\\n- You cannot pass refs as props between components (for that `forwardRef` is used).\\n- Avoid using them excessively\\n\\nAn example with `forwardRef` (if you need to pass a ref down to a child component):","import { useRef } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n    const inputRef = useRef(null);\\n}\\n\\n<input ref={inputRef} type=\\"text\\" />\\n\\nconst focusInput = () => {\\n    inputRef.current.focus(); // Setting focus on the input\\n};\\n\\nclass MyComponent extends React.Component {\\n    constructor(props) {\\n        super(props);\\n        this.inputRef = React.createRef();\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n<input ref={this.inputRef} type=\\"text\\" />\\n\\n// Somewhere in a method:\\nthis.inputRef.current.focus();\\n\\n<input ref={(el) => { this.inputRef = el; }} />\\n\\nconst Child = React.forwardRef((props, ref) => (\\n    <input ref={ref} {...props} />\\n));\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n    const inputRef = useRef();\\n    return <Child ref={inputRef} />;\\n}"],[2080,"`componentDidCatch` is a React class method for catching errors in child components. It is used in Error Boundaries in order to show a fallback UI instead of the whole application crashing.","`componentDidCatch` is a React class method for catching errors in child components. It is used in Error Boundaries in order to show a fallback UI instead of the whole application crashing.\\n\\nWhere it is used:\\n- Only in class components (there is no equivalent in functional ones yet).\\n- It does not catch errors in event handlers, in asynchronous code (`setTimeout`, for example) or in SSR.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe alternatives for functional components:\\nWhile React does not support `componentDidCatch` in hooks, you can:\\n- Use libraries such as `react-error-boundary`.\\n- Wrap your code in `try/catch` for synchronous operations.\\n\\nPractical uses:\\n- Showing the user an error message instead of a \\"white screen\\".\\n- Sending error logs to the server (to Sentry, for example).","class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {\\n    state = { hasError: false };\\n\\n    componentDidCatch(error, errorInfo) {\\n        this.setState({ hasError: true });\\n        console.error(\\"Error:\\", error, errorInfo);\\n    }\\n\\n    render() {\\n        if (this.state.hasError) {\\n            return <h1>Something went wrong.</h1>;\\n        }\\n        return this.props.children;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n// Usage:\\n<ErrorBoundary>\\n    <BuggyComponent />\\n</ErrorBoundary>"],[2081,"`useReducer` is a React hook that manages complex state by means of a reducer (a function that handles actions). It is worth using:\\n- When the state is complex (a great many related values — a form with validation, for example).\\n- When the update logic is non-trivial (many conditions, side effects).\\n- To optimize performance (reducers help you avoid unnecessary re-renders).\\n- As a simplified alternative to Redux (if you do not need global state).","1. How does `useReducer` work?\\n`useReducer` takes three arguments:\\n- The reducer — a function of the form `(state, action) => newState`, which determines how the state is to change in response to an action.\\n- The initial state.\\n- (Optionally) a function for lazily initializing the state.\\n\\nIt returns an array of two elements:\\n- The current state.\\n- The `dispatch` function, which sends actions to the reducer.\\n\\nA simple counter example:\\n\\nWhen should you use `useReducer` instead of `useState`?\\n`useState` is excellent for simple state — for holding a single value or a primitive data structure, for example. `useReducer` becomes the preferable choice, however, in the following cases:\\n- The state has a complex structure. If you need to manage a form with a great many fields, validation and dependent values, for example.\\n- The state-update logic is complex. If the changes of state depend on the previous state or require a great many conditions to be handled, a reducer helps you organize the code better.\\n- You need to minimize re-renders. `useReducer` lets you group several changes of state into one update, which can improve performance.\\n\\nAn example of a complex form with `useReducer`:\\n\\nWhen should you use `useReducer` instead of Redux?\\nRedux is a tool for managing an application\'s global state, but it is not always needed. `useReducer` can be an excellent alternative in the following cases:\\n- The state is local. If the state is needed only inside one component or a small group of components, `useReducer` will be simpler and more convenient.\\n- You do not need Redux\'s additional capabilities. If you do not require middleware (such as Redux Thunk or Saga), DevTools or time-travel debugging, for example.\\n- You want to avoid unnecessary complexity. Redux requires you to set up a store, actions and reducers, which can be excessive for small projects.\\n\\nWhen to choose Redux:\\n- Global state. If the state has to be available in many parts of the application.\\n- Complex side effects. Asynchronous requests handled through middleware, for example.\\n- The developer tools. The Redux DevTools provide powerful debugging capabilities.\\n\\nIn summary:\\n\\n- `useState` — for simple state (flags, counters, for example).\\n- `useReducer` — for complex state (forms, multi-step processes).\\n- Redux — for global state and advanced scenarios.","import { useReducer } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction counterReducer(state, action) {\\n    switch (action.type) {\\n        case \'increment\':\\n            return { count: state.count + 1 };\\n        case \'decrement\':\\n            return { count: state.count - 1 };\\n        default:\\n            return state;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n    const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(counterReducer, { count: 0 });\\n\\n    return (\\n        <div>\\n            <p>Count: {state.count}</p>\\n            <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: \'increment\' })}>+</button>\\n            <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: \'decrement\' })}>-</button>\\n        </div>\\n    );\\n}\\n\\nfunction formReducer(state, action) {\\n    switch (action.type) {\\n        case \'setUsername\':\\n            return { ...state, username: action.payload };\\n        case \'setPassword\':\\n            return { ...state, password: action.payload };\\n        case \'validate\':\\n            return { ...state, errors: validateForm(state) };\\n        case \'submit\':\\n            return { ...state, isSubmitting: true };\\n        default:\\n            return state;\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nfunction Form() {\\n    const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(formReducer, {\\n        username: \'\',\\n        password: \'\',\\n        errors: {},\\n        isSubmitting: false,\\n   });\\n\\n    const handleSubmit = (e) => {\\n        e.preventDefault();\\n        dispatch({ type: \'validate\' });\\n        if (Object.keys(state.errors).length === 0) {\\n            dispatch({ type: \'submit\' });\\n        }\\n   };\\n\\n    return (\\n        <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>\\n            <input\\n              value={state.username}\\n              onChange={(e) => dispatch({ type: \'setUsername\', payload: e.target.value })}\\n            />\\n            {state.errors.username && <p>{state.errors.username}</p>}\\n            <button disabled={state.isSubmitting}>Submit</button>\\n        </form>\\n    );\\n}"],[2082,"In functional components you can subscribe to events using `useEffect` for side effects, or by adding handlers to elements directly (`onClick`, for example). To unsubscribe from events (`resize`, `scroll`, for example) you have to return a clean-up function from `useEffect`.","1. Subscribing to DOM events (a click or a scroll, for example):\\n\\n2. Custom events (WebSocket, EventEmitter, for example):\\n\\n3. React events (`onClick`, `onChange`, for example):\\n\\nIn summary:\\n- For DOM events (`resize`, `scroll`) use `useEffect` plus an unsubscribe.\\n- For custom events (`WebSocket`, `EventEmitter`) `useEffect` is also needed.\\n- For React events (`onClick`, `onSubmit`) the handlers are attached directly.","import { useEffect } from \'react\';\\n\\nfunction MyComponent() {\\n    const handleClick = () => {\\n        console.log(\'A click!\');\\n    };\\n\\n    useEffect(() => {\\n        window.addEventListener(\'resize\', handleResize);\\n        return () => window.removeEventListener(\'resize\', handleResize); // Unsubscribing\\n    }, []);\\n\\n    return <button onClick={handleClick}>Click me</button>;\\n}\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n    const subscription = someEventEmitter.on(\'update\', handleUpdate);\\n    return () => subscription.unsubscribe(); // Unsubscribing\\n}, []);\\n\\nfunction Form() {\\n    const handleSubmit = (e) => {\\n        e.preventDefault();\\n        console.log(\'The form was submitted!\');\\n    };\\n\\n    return <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>...</form>;\\n}"],[2083,"A change of props causes a component to re-render. By default, when a parent re-renders its child components re-render too, even if their props have not changed. To avoid that, memoization is used (`React.memo`, `shouldComponentUpdate`, `PureComponent`).","1. The standard behaviour:\\n\\n- If the parent component updates the props, the child component re-renders.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\n2. Optimization (in order to avoid unnecessary re-renders):\\n\\n- `React.memo` (for functional components):\\n\\n- `shouldComponentUpdate` (for class components):\\n\\n3. When do props not cause a re-render?\\n\\n- If the prop is referentially the same (if an object has not changed, for example, even though its contents may have).\\n- If the component does not use the prop in `render()`.\\n\\nIn summary:\\n- Props cause a re-render by default.\\n- You can optimize that through `React.memo` or `shouldComponentUpdate`.","function Parent() {\\n    const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n    return <Child value={count} />; // Child re-renders on every change of count\\n}\\n\\nconst Child = React.memo(({ value }) => {\\n    return <div>{value}</div>;\\n});\\n\\nclass Child extends React.Component {\\n    shouldComponentUpdate(nextProps) {\\n        return nextProps.value !== this.props.value; // We re-render only if value has changed\\n    }\\n    render() { ... }\\n}"],[2084,"Yes, it can:\\n- Through a change of props (if the parent passes new data).\\n- Through a forced re-render (`forceUpdate` in classes, or a hack with `useReducer`).\\n- Through external stores (Redux, the Context API).","1. Through props (the parent component):\\n\\n2. Through `forceUpdate` (only in class components):\\n\\n3. Through `useReducer` (functional components):\\n\\n4. Through external stores (Redux, MobX, Context):\\n\\nIn summary:\\n- The main way is a change of `state` or `props`.\\n- The emergency cases are `forceUpdate` or `useReducer`.\\n- Global management is through stores (Redux, Context).","function Parent() {\\n    const [timestamp, setTimestamp] = useState(Date.now());\\n    return <Child key={timestamp} />; // A forced update of Child\\n}\\n\\nclass MyComponent extends React.Component {\\n    handleUpdate = () => {\\n        this.forceUpdate(); // A forced re-render\\n    };\\n    render() { ... }\\n}\\n\\nconst [, forceUpdate] = useReducer(x => x + 1, 0);\\nforceUpdate(); // It will cause a re-render\\n\\nconst { update } = useContext(MyContext);\\nupdate(newData); // It will update every subscribed component"],[2085,"Yes, but it is not worth doing. React lets you use random `key`s (`Math.random()` or `crypto.randomUUID()`, for example), but that leads to:\\n- A loss of performance (React will not be able to compare the elements properly).\\n- Bugs in how the components work (an unexpected re-render of form fields, for example).\\n- It is better to use stable unique values (IDs from the data, hashes, or indexes — with care).","Why are `key`s needed in React?\\nReact uses `key`s in order to track changes in lists and to understand which elements need updating and which can be reused.\\n\\nThe problems with random `key`s\\nIf you generate `key`s dynamically (`key={Math.random()}`, for example):\\n- A loss of the components\' state\\n- On a re-render React will see the new `key`s and decide that these are new elements rather than updated ones.\\n- If you have a form in a list, for example, its state will be reset.\\n\\n- Reduced performance\\n- React will not be able to optimize the updates, since the `key`s change every time.\\n\\nWhat should you use instead of random `key`s?\\n\\n- Unique IDs from the data (the best option):\\n\\n- Hashes of the content (if there are no IDs):\\n\\n- Array indexes (only if the list is static and there is no alternative):\\n\\nAn example of the bug with random `key`s","{items.map(item => <div key={item.id}>{item.name}</div>)}\\n\\nkey={`${item.name}-${item.price}`}\\n\\n{items.map((item, index) => <div key={index}>{item.name}</div>)}\\n\\n// Bad: the state is reset on every render\\n{items.map(item => (\\n    <input key={Math.random()} defaultValue={item.value} />\\n))}\\n\\n// Right: the keys are stable\\n{items.map(item => (\\n    <input key={item.id} defaultValue={item.value} />\\n))}"],[2086,"No, a Web Worker cannot change the DOM or render components directly. It runs in an isolated thread with no access to `document` or `window`. You can, however:\\n- Pass data to the main thread, where React will update the interface.\\n- Use proxy libraries such as `comlink` in order to make exchanging data easier.","Web Workers run code in the background but have no access to the DOM, because of the browsers\' security rules. An example of a way round it:\\n\\nStep 1: the Worker computes the data\\n\\nStep 2: the main thread updates React\\n\\nThe alternatives:\\n\\n- A Service Worker + a Virtual DOM: you can pre-render the components in the Worker and pass the markup over as a string.\\n- Off-screen rendering: in a Canvas through `OffscreenCanvas`, for example (for graphics only).","// worker.js  \\nonmessage = (e) => {  \\n  const result = heavyCalculation(e.data);  \\n  postMessage({ type: \'UPDATE_UI\', payload: result });  \\n};\\n\\n// main.js  \\nconst worker = new Worker(\'worker.js\');  \\nworker.onmessage = (e) => {  \\n  if (e.data.type === \'UPDATE_UI\') {  \\n    setState(e.data.payload); // We update React\'s state  \\n   }  \\n};"],[2087,"FSD (Feature-Sliced Design) and SOLID solve similar problems — creating clear, scalable code. The main parallels:\\n- The Single Responsibility Principle (SRP): each slice (feature) in FSD is responsible for one area.\\n- The Open/Closed Principle (OCP): FSD lets you extend functionality without changing the existing code.\\n- Dependency Inversion (DIP): FSD\'s layers (shared, entities, features) depend on abstractions rather than on one another.","1. Single Responsibility (SRP)\\n\\n- In FSD: each module (`/features/auth`, `/entities/user`) contains logic only for its own purpose.\\n\\n2. The Open-Closed Principle (OCP)\\n\\n- In FSD: new features are added as separate slices rather than as edits to the old ones.\\n- An example: adding the `/features/payments` module requires no changes in `/features/orders`.\\n\\n3. Dependency Inversion (DIP)\\n\\n- In FSD: the shared utilities (`/shared/lib`) do not depend on the features; the features use them through interfaces.\\n- An example:\\n\\nConclusion: FSD is the application of SOLID to the frontend. It divides code into layers, minimizes the connections between modules and makes maintenance easier.","/features/profile  \\n  ├── api.ts    // Requests to the server  \\n  ├── ui.tsx    // The components  \\n  └── model.ts  // The business logic\\n\\n// features/auth/model.ts  \\nimport { HttpClient } from \'@/shared/lib/api\'; // A dependency on an abstraction"],[2088,"- Global variables — they do not trigger a re-render, and changes are hard to track.\\n- Context — convenient for passing data without prop drilling.\\n- A State Manager (Redux, MobX) — predictability, time travel (debugging), scalability.","1. The problem with global variables\\n\\nThe problems:\\n- React will not learn that the variable has changed → there is no re-render.\\n- Keeping control of the dependencies is difficult.\\n\\n2. The solution: React Context\\nIt passes data through the component tree without passing props explicitly.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe advantages:\\n- We avoid prop drilling (A → B → C → D).\\n- Local state at the level of the application.\\n\\nThe disadvantages:\\n- Every consumer re-renders when it changes.\\n\\n3. A State Manager (Redux, MobX, Zustand)\\nA Redux example:\\n\\nWhy?\\n- Centralized state — a single source of truth.\\n- Developer tools — logging, undoing actions.\\n- Optimizations — targeted subscriptions to changes.\\n\\nConclusion:\\n- For small projects Context is enough.\\n- For complex ones — Redux/Zustand.\\n- Global variables — only for static data.","let user = null;  \\n\\n// Component A  \\nuser = { name: \'Alice\' };  \\n\\n// Component B  \\nconsole.log(user); // Sometimes current, sometimes not\\n\\nconst UserContext = createContext();  \\n\\nfunction App() {  \\n    const [user, setUser] = useState(null);  \\n    return (  \\n        <UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>  \\n            <Header />  \\n        </UserContext.Provider>  \\n    );  \\n}  \\n\\nfunction Header() {  \\n    const { user } = useContext(UserContext);  \\n    return <div>{user?.name}</div>;  \\n}\\n\\n// The store  \\nconst store = configureStore({ reducer: userReducer });  \\n\\n// A component  \\nconst user = useSelector(state => state.user);"],[2089,"No, a long `fetch` will not block the interface, because it runs asynchronously. JavaScript does not \\"wait\\" for the response — the request is sent in the background while the main thread goes on working.","The reasons the interface is not blocked:\\n- `fetch` is an asynchronous operation. It is performed by a browser API outside the main thread.\\n- The event loop handles the callbacks once the response has been received. Until the response arrives, the thread is free for other tasks (animations, clicks, for example).\\n\\nAn example in React:\\n\\nWhat happens under the hood:\\n- The browser sends the request and frees the main thread.\\n- When the response arrives, the callback (`then`) is put in the microtask queue.\\n- The event loop runs it as soon as the call stack is empty (once the current render has finished, for example).\\n\\nIf `fetch` were synchronous (like the old `XMLHttpRequest` with the flag `async: false`), it would block the interface. The modern `fetch` is always asynchronous, however.\\n\\nImportant:\\n- A long `fetch` does not block the interface, but it can slow other requests down (if the server or the browser limits the number of simultaneous connections).\\n- To cancel long requests, use an `AbortController`.","useEffect(() => {\\n  fetch(\'https://example.com/slow-api\')\\n    .then(response => response.json())\\n    .then(data => setData(data)); // The callback will run once the response arrives\\n}, []);"],[2090,"React can slow down if:\\n- Heavy synchronous computations are performed during rendering (processing large arrays, for example).\\n- The state is updated frequently (`setState` in a loop or without optimization).\\n- The components are not memoized (`React.memo`, `useMemo` and `useCallback` are not used where they should be).\\n- Long operations are performed in useEffect without being broken into frames (animations without `requestAnimationFrame`, for example).","React uses a virtual DOM and a reconciliation (diffing) algorithm in order to minimize the actual updates to the DOM. If the rendering of the components itself takes a long time, however, that leads to lag.\\n\\nThe main causes:\\n\\n- Synchronous computations in the render\\n\\nThe solution: move the computations into `useMemo` or a Web Worker, or break them into parts.\\n- Frequent state updates\\n\\nThe solution: update the state in a single call (`setCount(1000)`).\\n- Unoptimized lists\\n\\nThe solution: use `React.memo(Child)` or `useMemo` for the list.\\n- Long effects\\n\\nThe solution: break them into asynchronous tasks or use `setTimeout`/`requestIdleCallback`.","const HeavyComponent = () => {\\n  const data = processLargeArray(/* 1M items */); // It blocks the thread\\n  return <div>{data.length} items</div>;\\n};\\n\\nconst [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  for (let i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {\\n    setCount(prev => prev + 1); // 1000 re-renders!\\n  }\\n}, []);\\n\\n{items.map(item => <Child key={item.id} data={item} />)} // The child is not memoized\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  while (condition) { /* An infinite loop */ } // It hangs\\n}, []);"],[2091,"The main hooks:\\n- `useState` — holding a component\'s state.\\n- `useEffect` — side effects (requests, subscriptions).\\n- `useContext` — access to a context without props.\\n- `useRef` — references to the DOM or to mutable values.\\n- `useMemo` / `useCallback` — optimizing computations and functions.","1. `useState`\\nManaging a component\'s state.\\n\\n2. `useEffect`\\nFor requests, timers and subscriptions.\\n\\n3. `useContext`\\nAccess to global data (the theme, the authorization).\\n\\n4. `useRef`\\n\\n- For the DOM:\\n\\n- For mutable values (with no re-render):\\n\\n5. `useMemo` / `useCallback`\\n\\n- Caching values:\\n\\n- Caching functions:","const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n<button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>{count}</button>\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  fetchData().then(data => setData(data));\\n  return () => { /* Clean-up (cancelling the request, for example) */ };\\n}, [deps]);\\n\\nconst theme = useContext(ThemeContext);\\n<div style={{ color: theme.color }}>Text</div>\\n\\nconst inputRef = useRef();\\n<input ref={inputRef} />;\\n\\nconst timerId = useRef();\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  timerId.current = setInterval(() => {}, 1000);\\n}, []);\\n\\nconst filteredList = useMemo(() => (\\n  list.filter(item => item.price > 100)\\n), [list]);\\n\\nconst handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n  console.log(\'Clicked!\');\\n}, []);"],[2092,"What matters:\\n- Clean-up in the `return` — otherwise the interval will go on running after the component has been removed.\\n- An empty array of dependencies `[]` — so that the effect runs only on mounting.","The correct implementation:\\n\\nMistakes and subtleties:\\n\\n- Without clean-up → a memory leak, if the component is removed.\\n- The dependencies in `[]` — if you add variables, the effect will restart when they change.\\n- Using `useState` inside the interval:\\n\\nThe solution:\\n\\nConclusion: always clean your timers up and bear closures in mind!","import { useEffect } from \'react\';\\n\\nconst TimerComponent = () => {\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const intervalId = setInterval(() => {\\n      console.log(\'Hello world\');\\n    }, 10000);\\n\\n    return () => {\\n      clearInterval(intervalId); // Stopping it on unmounting\\n    };\\n  }, []); // An empty array = on mounting only\\n\\n  return <div>Check console!</div>;\\n};\\n\\nconst [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  const id = setInterval(() => {\\n    setCount(count + 1); // It will always use the initial value of `count`!\\n  }, 1000);\\n  return () => clearInterval(id);\\n}, []); // Wrong!\\n\\nsetCount(prev => prev + 1); // Use the functional form"],[2093,"In React 18 almost every capability of class components can be implemented in functional ones by means of hooks. The one exception is Error Boundaries (catching errors in child components). For now they can be created only through class components or by using ready-made npm packages.","Functional components with hooks in React 18 replace class components entirely. Classes used to be needed for state, for the lifecycle (`componentDidMount`, `componentWillUnmount` and others) and for refs (`this.refs`). All of that is now done through hooks:\\n- State → `useState`, `useReducer`\\n- The lifecycle → `useEffect`, `useLayoutEffect`\\n- Context → `useContext`\\n- Refs → `useRef`\\n\\nThere is one exception, however — Error Boundaries. This is a special mechanism for catching errors in child components (if a component crashes with an error, for example). React has no hook for this yet, so Error Boundaries can be created only through a class component with the `componentDidCatch` and `getDerivedStateFromError` methods.\\n\\nAn example of a class Error Boundary\\n\\nCan you do without classes?\\n\\n- Yes, if you use ready-made solutions such as `react-error-boundary`. Inside such packages a class is nevertheless used.\\n- No, if you write it from scratch — without classes or npm packages it cannot be done.\\n\\nWill Error Boundaries come to functional components?\\nReact has not added a hook such as `useErrorBoundary` yet, but it is possible in the future.\\n\\nConclusion\\nIn React 18 there are no features other than Error Boundaries that cannot be implemented in functional components. Everything else is covered by hooks. If you need to catch errors in the component tree, for now you will have to use classes or ready-made libraries.","class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {\\n  state = { hasError: false };\\n  static getDerivedStateFromError() { return { hasError: true }; }\\n  componentDidCatch(error, info) { console.log(error, info); }\\n  render() { return this.state.hasError ? \\"An error!\\" : this.props.children; }\\n}\\n\\n// Usage:\\n<ErrorBoundary>\\n  <MyComponent />\\n</ErrorBoundary>"],[2094,"Fiber is React\'s new architecture, which lets the rendering process be broken into small parts and distributed across frames, so that the interface stays responsive. It represents each element of the tree as a \\"fiber\\" structure with references to its parent, its children and its siblings. That gives the ability to pause, resume and cancel rendering.","The Fiber algorithm is React\'s internal implementation for working with the virtual DOM, optimized for updating the UI asynchronously. Its aim is to make rendering interruptible and incremental, so that the user interface does not \\"freeze\\" during large updates.\\n\\nThe main principles:\\n\\n- Dividing the work into chunks\\nReact breaks the rendering process into small tasks (units of work) that can be performed in parts between frames.\\n- Update priorities\\nState updates have different priorities (typing text, for example, is handled faster than loading data).\\n- Interruptibility\\nIf a more important task arrives, React can pause the current update and come back to it later.\\n- The Fiber tree\\nEach node is an object with information about the type of the component, its props, its state and the references to its parent, its child and its sibling.\\n\\nAn example of a simplified fiber structure:\\n\\nHow this affects the developer:\\n\\n- Better performance with large component trees.\\n- The ability to use Concurrent Mode and Suspense.\\n- A smoother UI during complex computations.\\n\\nConclusion: Fiber is needed so that React can update the UI incrementally and prioritize the important changes, keeping the interface responsive even during heavy operations.","const fiberNode = {\\n  type: \'div\',\\n  props: { children: \'Hello\' },\\n  stateNode: document.createElement(\'div\'),\\n  child: null,\\n  sibling: null,\\n  return: null\\n};"],[2095,"React groups several `setState` calls happening in the same synchronous event into a single update, in order to avoid unnecessary re-renders. This behaviour is called batching, and it works by default in React\'s event handlers; in asynchronous code it used to require `flushSync` or `unstable_batchedUpdates` to be called by hand.","In React, when `setState` is called the component does not update instantly. Instead React combines (batches) several updates in order to make a single re-render.\\n\\nHow it works:\\n\\n- Synchronous events\\nIn React\'s event handlers every `setState` call will be combined.\\n- Asynchronous code\\nBefore React 18 batching did not work in promises, timers or native events, and `unstable_batchedUpdates` had to be used.\\n- React 18\\nBatching is now enabled by default for asynchronous code too.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nConclusion: batching helps improve performance by combining updates and cutting down the number of unnecessary re-renders.","function Example() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);\\n  const [value, setValue] = React.useState(\'\');\\n\\n  function handleClick() {\\n    setCount(c => c + 1);\\n    setValue(\'Updated\');\\n    // There will be one render, not two\\n  }\\n\\n  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Click</button>;\\n}"],[2096,"React components can be tested by means of test runners (Jest) and rendering libraries (React Testing Library, Enzyme). They let you render a component in an isolated environment and check its behaviour through tests.","Testing React components without running the project is possible thanks to libraries that render components into a virtual DOM.\\n\\nThe main steps:\\n\\n- Choosing a test runner\\nJest is the usual choice.\\n- Rendering the component\\n- `React Testing Library` — for testing from the user\'s point of view (finding things by text, by role and so on).\\n- `Enzyme` — for testing a component\'s internal structure.\\n\\n- Checking the result\\nUsing methods such as `getByText` and `queryByRole` to find elements.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nConclusion: you can test UI logic without running the project, using Jest + React Testing Library for testing that is as close to reality as possible.","import { render, screen } from \'@testing-library/react\';\\nimport MyButton from \'./MyButton\';\\n\\ntest(\'it renders the button text\', () => {\\n  render(<MyButton />);\\n  expect(screen.getByText(/Click me/i)).toBeInTheDocument();\\n});"],[2097,"A single registry of versions is normally used, or shared dependencies are loaded through a CDN or module federation. It is important to avoid duplicating large libraries in every micro-frontend.","The approaches:\\n\\n- Shared dependencies\\n- In Module Federation (Webpack 5) you can declare React as `shared`.\\n\\n- A CDN\\n- Loading the dependencies globally and using them in every micro-frontend.\\n\\n- A monorepo\\n- A shared codebase with a single `package.json`.\\n\\n- Lock files\\n- A single lock file for every micro-frontend.\\n\\nAn example of Module Federation:\\n\\nConclusion: a single version of the dependencies reduces the weight of the bundles and prevents bugs caused by API incompatibility.","// host webpack config\\nshared: {\\n  react: { singleton: true, requiredVersion: \'^18.0.0\' },\\n  \'react-dom\': { singleton: true }\\n}"],[2098,"React 18 added automatic batching, a new rendering model through Concurrent Mode, and improvements to Fiber. This makes updates smoother and more efficient.","What is new in React 18:\\n\\n- Automatic batching\\n- It works in asynchronous callbacks too.\\n\\n- Concurrent Features\\n- The ability to prioritize rendering.\\n\\n- `useId`\\n- The generation of stable IDs.\\n\\n- Support for Suspense in SSR\\n- Optimizations to Fiber\\n- The handling of priorities has been improved.\\n\\nConclusion: React 18 is oriented towards improving performance and the smoothness of the UI by means of automatic batching and Concurrent Mode.",null],[2099,"When the state or the props change, React compares the new virtual DOM against the previous one and makes the minimal changes to the real DOM.","The process:\\n\\n- The render is called\\n- The component\'s function is called again.\\n\\n- A new virtual DOM is created\\n- Comparison (reconciliation)\\n- React uses the Fiber algorithm to find the differences.\\n\\n- The real DOM is updated\\n- Only the parts that have changed are applied to the browser\'s DOM.\\n\\nConclusion: a re-render is a repeated call of the component with an update to the UI, but React updates only the elements that have changed, as an optimization.",null],[2100,"Hooks solved the problems of the complexity of classes and of duplicated code and logic. They make reusing code easier, make it more readable, and let you use state and the lifecycle in functional components.","The problems with class components:\\n- Complexity: a large volume of code because of `this`, the lifecycle methods and `bind`.\\n- Repeated logic: subscribing/unsubscribing in `componentDidMount` and `componentWillUnmount`, for example.\\n- Difficulty of testing: classes are harder to isolate and to test.\\n\\nHow hooks solved these problems:\\n- `useState`: it lets you use state without classes.\\n- `useEffect`: it combines `componentDidMount`, `componentDidUpdate` and `componentWillUnmount`.\\n- Custom hooks: they let you reuse logic between components.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nConclusion: hooks made development easier, reduced the amount of code and improved the reuse of logic.","// A class component\\nclass Counter extends React.Component {\\n  state = { count: 0 };\\n  increment = () => this.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 });\\n  render() { return <button onClick={this.increment}>{this.state.count}</button>; }\\n}\\n\\n// A functional component with hooks\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  const increment = () => setCount(count + 1);\\n  return <button onClick={increment}>{count}</button>;\\n}"]]');export{e as default};